QB.add(41,[{"id":41001,"t":"Antibiotics","s":"Which feature makes antibiotics different from most other drugs?","o":["They are given to large numbers of patients for short periods","They are usually taken every day for 15 to 20 years, like diabetes tablets","They act only in a single body site, such as the lungs","They are each specific for a single organism","They are never used to prevent infection"],"e":"Antibiotics differ from other drugs: they act in several body sites, are given to large numbers of patients for short periods (a course of about 7–10 days, versus 15–20 years of diabetes tablets), are used prophylactically and therapeutically, have activity that varies and is rarely single-organism specific, and resistance may affect the recipient and spread clonally or by gene transfer.","w":["That describes long-term drugs such as diabetes tablets.","One antibiotic can act in several body sites (e.g. amoxicillin for chest and ear infections).","Antibiotic activity is rarely single-organism specific.","Antibiotics are used prophylactically as well as therapeutically."],"k":"Antibiotics: many patients, short courses, several sites, prophylaxis and treatment.","rn":[["","How Are antibiotics different from other drugs?","How Are antibiotics different from other drugs?","Activity in several body sites; Large numbers of patients for short periods; Prophylactically and therapeutically; Activity against microorganisms varies and is rarely single organism specificDrug resistance may affect the recipient and may also disseminate clonally or by gene transfer"]]},{"id":41002,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"A patient is given an antibiotic just before surgery, with no current infection. What is this use called?","o":["Prophylactic","Therapeutic, because it treats an infection that is already present","Empirical, because the organism has already been identified","Bacteriostatic, because it only stops bacterial growth","Synergistic, because two drugs are combined"],"e":"Prophylactic means to prevent infection, for example antibiotics given before surgery. Therapeutic means treating an infection you already have. Antibiotics are used both ways, and the longer and more often they are used, the more resistance develops.","w":["Therapeutic use treats an existing infection.","Empirical treatment starts before the organism is known in an infected patient.","Bacteriostatic describes a drug's effect, not the reason for giving it.","Synergy describes combined drug effects."],"k":"Prophylactic = prevent; therapeutic = treat.","rn":[["","Antibiotic given before surgery is an example of what use?","Antibiotic given before surgery is an example of what use?","Prophylactic use"]]},{"id":41003,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"Two hospital trusts treat the same infection with different first-line antibiotics. Why, according to the lecture?","o":["Local infection patterns and resistance differ, so each trust has its own policy","National law requires every trust to use a different antibiotic","Antibiotics only work in the trust where they were developed","Bacteria in one region of the country are never resistant to any of the antibiotics used there","The trusts choose at random each year"],"e":"Each hospital trust has its own antibiotic policy, depending on which infections are common locally and on local resistance. For example, TB is more common in London than in Kent, and a recent rise in pneumococcal/meningitis infections changed policy in Kent.","w":["No such national law is described.","Antibiotics work regardless of where they were developed.","Resistance occurs everywhere, which is why local patterns matter.","Policies are based on local data, not chance."],"k":"Trust antibiotic policies reflect local infections and resistance.","rn":[["","Why do antibiotic choices vary between trusts or areas?","Why do antibiotic choices vary between trusts or areas?","Different prevalent infections; Different local resistance"]]},{"id":41004,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"Which of the following is NOT one of the ideal characteristics of an antibiotic listed in the lecture?","o":["Broad spectrum against every organism","Selective toxicity","Slow emergence of resistance","Non-toxic to the host","No interference with other drugs"],"e":"The ideal characteristics are selective toxicity, slow emergence of resistance, non-toxic to the host and no interference with other drugs; the slide asks whether this is possible. Broad spectrum is not an ideal: broader cover increases the risk of resistance and side effects.","w":["Selective toxicity is listed.","Slow emergence of resistance is listed.","Being non-toxic to the host is listed.","No interference with other drugs is listed."],"k":"Ideal: selective toxicity, slow resistance, non-toxic, no interactions.","rn":[["","What is the downside of broad spectrum antibiotics?","What is the downside of broad spectrum antibiotics?","Increased risk of resistance; Increased side effects"],["","Amoxicillin: spectrum?","Amoxicillin: spectrum?","Broad"],["","Tetracyclines: spectrum?","Tetracyclines: spectrum?","Broad"]]},{"id":41005,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"A patient phones asking for antibiotics for flu. Which ideal characteristic of antibiotic use does refusing protect most directly?","o":["Slow emergence of resistance","Selective toxicity of the drug for bacteria over host cells","Absence of drug interactions with the patient's medicines","Good absorption from the gastrointestinal tract","Activity in several body sites at once"],"e":"To keep resistance emerging slowly, antibiotics should only be given when absolutely necessary: not over the phone just because a patient asks, and not for flu. NHS advice is to prescribe only when absolutely necessary and not over the telephone.","w":["Selective toxicity concerns hitting bacteria rather than the host.","Interactions are checked when adding a drug, but this is about avoiding unnecessary use.","Absorption is a pharmacokinetic property, not the issue here.","Activity in several sites is a feature, not the reason for refusal."],"k":"Avoid unnecessary prescribing to slow resistance.","rn":[["","How do prescribers slow the emergence of resistance?","How do prescribers slow the emergence of resistance?","Only give when absolutely necessary"],["","What are the 4 ideal characteristics of an antibiotic?","What are the 4 ideal characteristics of an antibiotic?","Selective toxicity; Slow emergence of resistance; Non-toxic to host; No interference with other drugs"]]},{"id":41006,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"What is the basis of selective toxicity of antibiotics?","o":["Bacteria have targets we lack, as prokaryotes versus eukaryotic human cells","Bacteria and human cells are structurally identical, so dose alone determines toxicity","Antibiotics only work in the stomach","Human cells have a peptidoglycan wall that antibiotics avoid","Antibiotics attack the human nucleus"],"e":"Selective toxicity exploits differences in structure and metabolism between microorganisms and the host: prokaryotes (no nucleus) versus eukaryotes (nucleus), and differences between bacterial groups (Gram positive vs Gram negative). Anything bacteria have that we don't, such as a cell wall, different ribosomes or their own enzymes, is a safe target.","w":["They differ structurally, which is what makes selective toxicity possible.","Antibiotics act in several body sites.","Human cells have no peptidoglycan wall.","Attacking the human nucleus would harm the host."],"k":"Selective toxicity: target what bacteria have and we don't.","rn":[["","Are bacteria prokaryotes or eukaryotes? What about humans?","Are bacteria prokaryotes or eukaryotes? What about humans?","Bacteria: prokaryotes; Humans: eukaryotes (have a nucleus)"]]},{"id":41007,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a consideration when selecting an antibiotic?","o":["The colour and shape of the tablet","Likely pathogens","Pathogen susceptibility","Antimicrobial resistance patterns","Pharmacokinetics and patient factors"],"e":"Selecting an antibiotic considers likely pathogens, pathogen susceptibility to specific agents, antimicrobial resistance patterns, pharmacokinetics of the agent, and patient factors such as age and liver or kidney function.","w":["Likely pathogens are considered.","Susceptibility is considered.","Resistance patterns are considered.","Pharmacokinetics and patient factors are considered."],"k":"Choose by pathogen, susceptibility, resistance, pharmacokinetics, patient."},{"id":41008,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"An 80-year-old with liver failure needs an antibiotic that is broken down by the liver. What is the main concern?","o":["The drug may not be cleared, so levels build up and cause harm","The drug will work faster and more safely because the damaged liver no longer removes it","The drug will be excreted only in sweat","Liver failure makes bacteria resistant","No change is ever needed for older patients"],"e":"Patient factors and pharmacokinetics matter: doses differ between a 20-year-old and an 80-year-old, and in liver or kidney failure doses may need reducing. A drug broken down by the liver in a patient with liver failure is not cleared, so levels build up and cause harm, and the dose is reduced.","w":["Reduced clearance increases harm, not speed.","Excretion route is not changed to sweat.","Liver failure does not cause bacterial resistance.","Age and organ function do change dosing."],"k":"Organ failure → reduced clearance → reduce dose.","rn":[["","How is vancomycin dosed?","How is vancomycin dosed?","By drug levels in blood"],["","Amoxicillin: interaction?","Amoxicillin: interaction?","Raises levels of other protein bound drugs"],["","Why avoid giving clarithromycin with other drugs that cause arrhythmia?","Why avoid giving clarithromycin with other drugs that cause arrhythmia?","Clarithromycin may alter cardiac conduction; Arrhythmia risk adds up"]]},{"id":41009,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"Put the steps of empirical treatment in order: (P) switch to the antibiotic that suits the bacterium; (Q) start an antibiotic covering likely bacteria; (R) culture and sensitivity result returns; (S) send a culture sample to the lab.","o":["Q → S → R → P","S → R → Q → P","R → Q → S → P","Q → R → S → P","P → Q → S → R"],"e":"Empirical treatment is antibiotic therapy started before the organism is identified. A patient arrives with an infection; you start an antibiotic covering the likely bacteria straight away, send a culture sample, and when the culture and sensitivity result returns you switch to the antibiotic that suits that bacterium.","w":["Treatment starts before results, not after.","The result cannot come before the sample is sent.","The sample must be sent before the result returns.","Switching comes last, after the result."],"k":"Empirical: treat likely bugs → culture → result → switch.","rn":[["","After empirical treatment, what changes the antibiotic?","After empirical treatment, what changes the antibiotic?","Culture and sensitivity results"]]},{"id":41010,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","a":["empirical treatment","empirical","empiric","empiric treatment","empirical therapy"],"s":"What term describes antibiotic therapy started before the causative micro-organism has been identified?","e":"Empirical treatment is antibiotic therapy started before identification of the causative micro-organism is available; it is refined when culture and sensitivity results return.","k":"Empirical = before the organism is known.","rn":[["","What is meant by Empirical Treatment?","What is meant by Empirical Treatment?","Antibiotic started before the causative micro-organism is identified"]]},{"id":41011,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"Why is a narrow-spectrum antibiotic often preferred when the likely organism is known?","o":["Broader spectrum increases the risk of resistance and side effects","Narrow-spectrum drugs always kill every bacterial group, including atypicals","Broad-spectrum drugs cannot be absorbed","Narrow-spectrum drugs never cause allergy","Broad-spectrum drugs work only against viruses"],"e":"The groups covered are Gram positive, anaerobes, Gram negative and atypicals. Narrow-spectrum drugs cover one end; broad-spectrum drugs span across the groups. Broader spectrum means an increased risk of resistance and side effects, partly because good commensal bacteria are also hit.","w":["Narrow-spectrum drugs cover only some groups.","Many broad-spectrum drugs are well absorbed.","Allergy is not prevented by narrow spectrum (all β-lactams risk allergy).","Antibiotics act on bacteria."],"k":"Broader spectrum → more resistance and side effects.","f":"am08","rn":[["","What is the downside of broad spectrum antibiotics?","What is the downside of broad spectrum antibiotics?","Increased risk of resistance; Increased side effects"]]},{"id":41012,"t":"Mechanisms of Action","s":"Which pairing of antibiotic target and class is correct?","o":["RNA synthesis: rifampicin","Cell wall: macrolides","DNA replication: aminoglycosides","Antimetabolites: beta-lactams","Protein synthesis (50S): quinolones"],"e":"Targets: cell wall (beta-lactams, glycopeptides); protein synthesis 50S (macrolides); protein synthesis 30S (aminoglycosides, tetracyclines); DNA replication (quinolones, metronidazole); RNA synthesis (rifampicin); antimetabolites (trimethoprim, sulphonamides).","w":["Macrolides act on the 50S ribosome.","Aminoglycosides act on the 30S ribosome.","β-lactams act on the cell wall.","Quinolones act on DNA replication."],"k":"Rifampicin → RNA synthesis; macrolides → 50S; quinolones → DNA.","rn":[["","Which drug targets RNA synthesis?","Which drug targets RNA synthesis?","Rifampicin"],["","Rifampicin: mechanism of action?","Rifampicin: mechanism of action?","Binds RNA polymerase; Inhibits mRNA synthesis"]]},{"id":41013,"t":"Mechanisms of Action","s":"What is the difference between bactericidal and bacteriostatic drugs, and why does it matter?","o":["Bactericidal kills; bacteriostatic stops growth, so immunity must finish the job","Bactericidal stops growth only; bacteriostatic kills all bacteria and needs no immune help","Both kill bacteria in exactly the same way","Bacteriostatic drugs work only on viruses","Bactericidal drugs never need the immune system"],"e":"Bactericidal drugs kill bacteria. Bacteriostatic drugs do not kill them; they stop them growing (suppress them), and the immune system has to finish the job. With a bacteriostatic drug the infection can come back after the course ends, so choose carefully.","w":["This reverses the definitions.","They act differently.","Bacteriostatic drugs act on bacteria.","The point is that bacteriostatic drugs rely more on immunity."],"k":"Bactericidal kills; bacteriostatic suppresses (immunity finishes).","rn":[["","What is meant by Bacteriostatic?","What is meant by Bacteriostatic?","Suppresses bacteria; Does not kill them"],["","Why can infection return after a bacteriostatic antibiotic?","Why can infection return after a bacteriostatic antibiotic?","Bacteria are only suppressed, not killed"],["","What is meant by Bactericidal?","What is meant by Bactericidal?","Kills bacteria"]]},{"id":41014,"t":"Mechanisms of Action","s":"On this diagram of antibiotic targets, label 6 points to antimetabolites. Which drugs belong there?","o":["Trimethoprim and sulphonamides","Beta-lactams and glycopeptides","Quinolones and metronidazole","Macrolides only","Aminoglycosides and tetracyclines"],"e":"The figure labels 1 cell wall (beta-lactams, glycopeptides); 2 DNA replication (quinolones, metronidazole); 3 RNA synthesis (rifampicin); 4 protein synthesis (macrolides); 5 protein synthesis (aminoglycosides, tetracyclines); 6 antimetabolites (trimethoprim, sulphonamides).","w":["These act on the cell wall.","These act on DNA replication.","Macrolides act on protein synthesis.","These act on protein synthesis (30S)."],"k":"Antimetabolites: trimethoprim and sulphonamides.","f":"am09","rn":[["","Which 2 drugs are antimetabolites?","Which 2 drugs are antimetabolites?","Trimethoprim; Sulphonamides"]]},{"id":41015,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","a":["bacteriostatic"],"s":"What term describes an antibiotic that stops bacterial growth without killing the bacteria?","e":"A bacteriostatic antibiotic stops bacteria growing; the immune system finishes the job. A bactericidal antibiotic kills them.","k":"Bacteriostatic = stops growth.","rn":[["","Tetracyclines: bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Tetracyclines: bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Bacteriostatic"],["","What is meant by Bacteriostatic?","What is meant by Bacteriostatic?","Suppresses bacteria; Does not kill them"],["","Why can infection return after a bacteriostatic antibiotic?","Why can infection return after a bacteriostatic antibiotic?","Bacteria are only suppressed, not killed"]]},{"id":41016,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which statement about drugs that interfere with the cell wall or membrane is correct?","o":["They are generally bactericidal","They are generally bacteriostatic and rely on the immune system","They act on the bacterial 30S ribosome","They block folate synthesis","They target human cell membranes"],"e":"Drugs interfering with the membrane or cell wall/peptidoglycan synthesis include β-lactams, glyco-/lipopeptides, phosphonic acids (e.g. fosfomycin) and polymyxins (outer membrane). These are generally bactericidal.","w":["They are generally bactericidal.","The 30S ribosome is targeted by aminoglycosides and tetracyclines.","Folate synthesis is targeted by antimetabolites.","They target bacterial structures."],"k":"Cell wall/membrane drugs: generally bactericidal.","rn":[["","Are cell wall inhibitors bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Are cell wall inhibitors bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Generally bactericidal"]]},{"id":41017,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which pairing of β-lactam group and example is correct?","o":["Carbapenems: meropenem","Monobactams: amoxicillin","Cephalosporins: aztreonam","Mecillinams: imipenem","Penicillins: cefuroxime"],"e":"β-lactam groups: penicillins (benzylpenicillin, flucloxacillin, amoxicillin); cephalosporins (cephalexin, cefuroxime); mecillinams (pivmecillinam); carbapenems (imipenem, meropenem); monobactams (aztreonam).","w":["Amoxicillin is a penicillin.","Aztreonam is a monobactam.","Imipenem is a carbapenem.","Cefuroxime is a cephalosporin."],"k":"Meropenem and imipenem are carbapenems.","rn":[["","Imipenem and meropenem are examples of?","Imipenem and meropenem are examples of?","Carbapenems"]]},{"id":41018,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"An exam question asks which enzyme amoxicillin blocks. Which answer is correct?","o":["Penicillin-binding protein (transpeptidase)","DNA gyrase","RNA polymerase","Dihydrofolate reductase","β-lactamase"],"e":"β-lactams bind bacterial transpeptidases, enzymes essential for peptidoglycan synthesis; transpeptidases are the same as penicillin-binding proteins. The lecturer warned the answer may be written as penicillin-binding protein rather than transpeptidase.","w":["DNA gyrase is blocked by quinolones.","RNA polymerase is blocked by rifampicin.","Dihydrofolate reductase is blocked by trimethoprim.","β-lactamase destroys β-lactams; it is blocked by clavulanic acid."],"k":"Amoxicillin blocks transpeptidase = penicillin-binding protein.","rn":[["","What is the other name for transpeptidases?","What is the other name for transpeptidases?","Penicillin binding proteins (PBPs)"]]},{"id":41019,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Put the β-lactam mode of action in order: (P) cell lysis; (Q) β-lactam binds transpeptidase; (R) cell wall formation is inhibited; (S) cross-linking peptides cannot bind tetrapeptide side-chains.","o":["Q → S → R → P","S → Q → R → P","Q → R → S → P","R → S → Q → P","P → Q → S → R"],"e":"β-lactams bind bacterial transpeptidases (penicillin-binding proteins), preventing the cross-linking peptides binding to the tetrapeptide side-chains. Cell wall formation is inhibited, leading to cell lysis: bactericidal. The β-lactam ring is the functional unit.","w":["Binding to the enzyme comes first.","Cross-linking failure causes the wall defect.","Wall inhibition follows the cross-linking block.","Lysis is the final result."],"k":"β-lactam: bind PBP → no cross-links → weak wall → lysis.","rn":[["","How do beta lactams stop cross-linking?","How do beta lactams stop cross-linking?","Block cross-linking peptides; Binding tetrapeptide side-chains"]]},{"id":41020,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"In this peptidoglycan diagram, what do the red minus arrows indicate?","o":["Where β-lactams block cross-linking","Where vancomycin binds the D-Ala-D-Ala tails of the wall building blocks","Where β-lactamase cuts the ring","Where DNA gyrase acts","Where ribosomes make protein"],"e":"The figure labels 1 β-lactams preventing the cross-linking peptides from binding to the tetrapeptide side-chains; 2 the tetrapeptide side-chain; 3 peptide cross-links; 4 the peptidoglycan backbone (NAG and NAMA chains). The red minus arrows show where β-lactams block cross-linking.","w":["Vancomycin binding is not what the arrows show.","β-lactamase cutting is shown on another slide.","DNA gyrase acts on DNA.","Ribosomes are not in this figure."],"k":"β-lactams block peptide cross-links in peptidoglycan.","f":"am13","rn":[["","How do beta lactams stop cross-linking?","How do beta lactams stop cross-linking?","Block cross-linking peptides; Binding tetrapeptide side-chains"]]},{"id":41021,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","a":["transpeptidase","transpeptidases","penicillin-binding protein","penicillin binding protein","pbp","pbps"],"s":"Name the bacterial enzyme, also called a penicillin-binding protein, that builds the peptide cross-links in peptidoglycan.","e":"Transpeptidases, also called penicillin-binding proteins, build the peptide cross-links between NAG–NAM chains. β-lactams bind them, blocking cross-linking.","k":"Transpeptidase = PBP.","rn":[["","Which step do beta lactams block in murein formation?","Which step do beta lactams block in murein formation?","TP (transpeptidase) step: cross-linking"]]},{"id":41022,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"What is the functional unit of all β-lactam antibiotics?","o":["The β-lactam ring","The D-Ala-D-Ala tail","The 50S ribosomal subunit","The tetrahydrofolate molecule","The mycolic acid layer"],"e":"They are called β-lactams because they all contain the β-lactam ring, which is the functional unit. You do not need to memorise the structure. In penicillin it is fused to a second ring.","w":["D-Ala-D-Ala is the target of vancomycin.","The 50S subunit is a ribosomal target.","Tetrahydrofolate is part of the folate pathway.","Mycolic acid is in mycobacterial walls."],"k":"β-lactam ring = functional unit.","rn":[["","What is the functional unit of beta lactams?","What is the functional unit of beta lactams?","Beta lactam ring"]]},{"id":41023,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"What is the main mechanism of bacterial resistance to β-lactams?","o":["β-lactamases that hydrolyse the β-lactam ring","Efflux of folate from the cell","Mutation of the 30S ribosomal subunit so the drug no longer binds","Binding of calcium in teeth","Increased production of NAG"],"e":"The main resistance to β-lactams is β-lactamases produced by bacteria, which catalyse hydrolysis of the β-lactam ring, making the antibiotic inactive. This can be overcome with β-lactamase inhibitors such as clavulanic acid.","w":["Folate efflux is not described.","Ribosomal mutation affects protein synthesis inhibitors.","Calcium binding is tetracycline chelation.","NAG production is not the resistance mechanism."],"k":"β-lactamases hydrolyse the β-lactam ring.","rn":[["","What do beta-lactamases do?","What do beta-lactamases do?","Hydrolyse the beta lactam ring; Antibiotic becomes inactive"]]},{"id":41024,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"A patient is not improving on amoxicillin, and a β-lactamase-producing organism is suspected. Which drug combination addresses this?","o":["Co-amoxiclav (amoxicillin + clavulanic acid)","Amoxicillin combined with a second penicillin such as penicillin V","Vancomycin + gentamicin","Trimethoprim alone","Metronidazole + rifampicin"],"e":"Clavulanic acid is a β-lactamase inhibitor: it does not kill bacteria itself but blocks the β-lactamase so the penicillin can work. Co-amoxiclav is amoxicillin plus clavulanic acid. β-lactamase-producing bacteria may be one reason a patient is not improving on amoxicillin.","w":["A second penicillin would also be destroyed by β-lactamase.","This pair adds kidney and ear toxicity and is not the targeted fix.","Trimethoprim does not address β-lactamase.","These are not β-lactamase inhibitors."],"k":"Co-amoxiclav: clavulanic acid protects amoxicillin from β-lactamase.","rn":[["","Co-amoxiclav = ?","Co-amoxiclav = ?","Amoxicillin + clavulanic acid"]]},{"id":41025,"t":"Mechanisms of Action","s":"Which statement about clavulanic acid is correct?","o":["It inhibits many β-lactamases but does not kill bacteria itself","It is a powerful bactericidal antibiotic on its own against β-lactamase producers","It inhibits DNA gyrase","It binds the 50S ribosome","It increases β-lactamase production"],"e":"Clavulanic acid has its own β-lactam ring and inhibits many β-lactamases. It does not kill bacteria itself; it blocks the β-lactamase so the penicillin (e.g. amoxicillin) can work.","w":["It does not kill bacteria itself.","DNA gyrase is blocked by quinolones.","50S is targeted by macrolides.","It inhibits β-lactamase."],"k":"Clavulanic acid: β-lactamase inhibitor, not an antibacterial itself.","rn":[["","What is meant by Bactericidal?","What is meant by Bactericidal?","Kills bacteria"],["","What is meant by Bacteriostatic?","What is meant by Bacteriostatic?","Suppresses bacteria; Does not kill them"],["","What is the main resistance to beta lactams?","What is the main resistance to beta lactams?","Beta-lactamases made by bacteria"]]},{"id":41026,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which β-lactam nucleus is described as β-lactamase resistant on the slide?","o":["Monobactam","Penicillin","Cephalosporin","Penicillin with amidase","None of them"],"e":"The slide shows penicillin and cephalosporin nuclei attacked by β-lactamase (and amidase on penicillin), monobactam as β-lactamase resistant, carbapenem with high resistance to β-lactamases, and clavulanic acid inhibiting many β-lactamases.","w":["Penicillin is attacked by β-lactamase.","Cephalosporin is attacked by β-lactamase.","Amidase also attacks penicillin.","Monobactams and carbapenems resist β-lactamase."],"k":"Monobactam resistant; carbapenem highly resistant; penicillin/cephalosporin attacked.","f":"am14","rn":[["","What are the 5 classes of beta lactams?","What are the 5 classes of beta lactams?","Penicillins; Cephalosporins; Mecillinams; Carbapenems; Monobactams"],["","Which beta lactam nucleus is beta-lactamase resistant?","Which beta lactam nucleus is beta-lactamase resistant?","Monobactam nucleus"]]},{"id":41027,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","a":["clavulanic acid","clavulanate"],"s":"Name the β-lactamase inhibitor combined with amoxicillin in co-amoxiclav.","e":"Clavulanic acid is the β-lactamase inhibitor in co-amoxiclav. It blocks many β-lactamases so amoxicillin can work.","k":"Co-amoxiclav = amoxicillin + clavulanic acid.","rn":[["","Co-amoxiclav = ?","Co-amoxiclav = ?","Amoxicillin + clavulanic acid"]]},{"id":41028,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Why were chemically modified penicillins developed from penicillin G (benzylpenicillin)?","o":["Penicillin G is poorly absorbed orally","Penicillin G is too toxic to humans","Penicillin G does not contain a β-lactam ring","Penicillin G works only against viruses","Penicillin G cannot be made in a lab"],"e":"Natural penicillin G (benzylpenicillin) has poor oral absorption, which is why chemically modified penicillins were made: penicillin V (oral, but several times a day), aminopenicillins such as amoxicillin and ampicillin (longer half-lives, broader spectrum), β-lactamase-resistant ones (flucloxacillin, temocillin) and extended-spectrum ones (ticarcillin, piperacillin) active against Pseudomonas.","w":["Penicillin is not toxic to humans at a therapeutic dose.","All penicillins contain the β-lactam ring.","Penicillins act on bacteria.","The issue is absorption, not production."],"k":"Penicillin G: poor oral absorption → modified penicillins.","rn":[["","Is gentamicin active when given orally?","Is gentamicin active when given orally?","No; Poorly absorbed; given parenterally"]]},{"id":41029,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"A patient with a Pseudomonas wound infection needs a penicillin. Which type is appropriate?","o":["An extended-spectrum penicillin such as piperacillin","Natural penicillin G given by mouth several times a day","Penicillin V","Flucloxacillin","Meticillin"],"e":"Many penicillins have no useful activity against Pseudomonas (a Gram-negative rod causing wound infections), so there are special antipseudomonal, extended-spectrum penicillins such as ticarcillin and piperacillin. Different species have different transpeptidases, which is why there are so many penicillins.","w":["Penicillin G is poorly absorbed orally and not antipseudomonal.","Penicillin V is not antipseudomonal.","Flucloxacillin is β-lactamase-resistant, not antipseudomonal.","Meticillin is not used clinically."],"k":"Pseudomonas: piperacillin (extended spectrum).","rn":[["","Which extended-spectrum penicillins are active in Pseudomonas?","Which extended-spectrum penicillins are active in Pseudomonas?","Ticarcillin; Piperacillin"]]},{"id":41030,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"What must always be asked before prescribing any β-lactam?","o":["Whether the patient has a penicillin allergy","Whether the patient's urine is orange","Whether the patient has ever had tuberculosis treated with rifampicin","Whether the patient's teeth are discoloured","Whether the patient has had chickenpox"],"e":"Allergy is important for all β-lactams. Always ask about penicillin allergy in the history before prescribing; if you don't and the patient reacts, the prescriber is responsible. Common ADRs also include damage to commensal flora and rashes.","w":["Orange secretions relate to rifampicin.","TB history is not the key β-lactam question.","Tooth discolouration relates to tetracyclines.","Chickenpox history is not relevant here."],"k":"Always ask about penicillin allergy.","rn":[["","Why must a prescriber ask about allergy before prescribing an antibiotic?","Why must a prescriber ask about allergy before prescribing an antibiotic?","Allergy to penicillins is common; A reaction can happen suddenly"],["","When are macrolides prescribed instead of penicillins?","When are macrolides prescribed instead of penicillins?","Penicillin allergy; Penicillin resistance"]]},{"id":41031,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which statement about the core drug amoxicillin is correct?","o":["It has good GI absorption, is not significantly metabolised and is excreted in urine","It is poorly absorbed from the gut, extensively metabolised by the liver and excreted mainly in bile","It inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit","It is active only against Gram-positive bacteria including MRSA","It is a strong inducer of CYP450 enzymes"],"e":"Amoxicillin inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis. It is broad spectrum (Streptococcus pyogenes, pneumococcal and coliform infections), with good GI absorption, no significant metabolism and excretion in urine. Adverse effects: allergy/hypersensitivity (rashes) and damage to commensal flora. It can increase levels of other protein-bound drugs.","w":["Amoxicillin has good GI absorption and urinary excretion; bile excretion describes clarithromycin.","50S inhibition describes macrolides such as clarithromycin.","Gram-positive-only activity including MRSA describes vancomycin.","CYP450 induction describes rifampicin."],"k":"Amoxicillin: well absorbed, not metabolised, urine excretion.","rn":[["","Amoxicillin: GI absorption?","Amoxicillin: GI absorption?","Good"]]},{"id":41032,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which infections are listed for amoxicillin's broad-spectrum activity?","o":["Streptococcus pyogenes, pneumococcal and coliform infections","MRSA bacteraemia and Clostridium difficile diarrhoea only","Tuberculosis and leprosy","Mycoplasma only","Pseudomonas wound infections only"],"e":"Amoxicillin is broad spectrum: Streptococcus pyogenes (sore throat, skin infections), pneumococcal infections (respiratory tract) and coliform infections (urinary tract infections).","w":["MRSA and C. difficile relate to vancomycin.","TB and leprosy relate to rifampicin.","Mycoplasma is covered by clarithromycin.","Pseudomonas needs extended-spectrum penicillins."],"k":"Amoxicillin covers S. pyogenes, pneumococci, coliforms.","rn":[["","What 3 infections does amoxicillin treat?","What 3 infections does amoxicillin treat?","S. pyogenes (sore throat, skin); Pneumococcal (respiratory tract); Coliform (UTIs)"]]},{"id":41033,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"A patient on amoxicillin develops sore white patches in the mouth. Which mechanism explains this?","o":["Commensal bacteria are killed, letting Candida flare up as an opportunistic infection","Amoxicillin directly irritates the oral mucosa, causing an allergic white rash","Amoxicillin causes tooth discolouration","Amoxicillin turns saliva orange","Amoxicillin destroys the β-lactam ring"],"e":"Commensal bacteria normally keep other organisms, such as fungi, suppressed. An antibiotic kills the good bacteria, and suppressed organisms flare up: an opportunistic infection, for example Candida albicans causing oral thrush. Damage to commensal flora is a listed adverse effect of amoxicillin.","w":["Drugs do not infect.","Tooth discolouration relates to tetracyclines.","Orange saliva relates to rifampicin.","β-lactamase destroys the ring, not amoxicillin itself."],"k":"Antibiotics kill commensals → opportunistic Candida.","rn":[["","What is meant by Opportunistic Infection?","What is meant by Opportunistic Infection?","Normally suppressed microbes flare up; After protective commensals are killed"]]},{"id":41034,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which interaction is listed for amoxicillin?","o":["It can increase levels of other protein-bound drugs","It strongly induces CYP450 enzymes and so lowers the levels of other drugs","It adds to kidney toxicity with gentamicin","It prolongs cardiac conduction with other drugs","It has no interactions at all"],"e":"Amoxicillin's listed interaction: it can increase levels of other protein-bound drugs. The full interaction list is long and not needed now. CYP450 induction is rifampicin; nephrotoxic additive effects are vancomycin/gentamicin; cardiac conduction effects are clarithromycin.","w":["CYP450 induction is rifampicin.","Nephrotoxicity is vancomycin and aminoglycosides.","Cardiac conduction effects are clarithromycin.","An interaction is listed."],"k":"Amoxicillin raises other protein-bound drug levels.","rn":[["","Amoxicillin: interaction?","Amoxicillin: interaction?","Raises levels of other protein bound drugs"]]},{"id":41035,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"How are cephalosporins divided into generations?","o":["By which bacteria they work on (Gram positive, Gram negative or both)","By their colour in solution","By whether they are licensed for use in winter or summer","By the year each patient was born","By the number of rings in penicillin"],"e":"Cephalosporins are very commonly used and split into generations by which bacteria they work on. Earlier generations tend to be more active against Gram positives, middle generations more against Gram negatives with increased β-lactamase resistance, and the 5th generation (ceftaroline) is active against Gram positives including MRSA. You do not need the full table yet.","w":["Colour is not the basis.","Season is not the basis.","Patient birth year is irrelevant.","Generations are not defined by penicillin rings."],"k":"Cephalosporin generations reflect spectrum.","rn":[["","Glycopeptides are only active against what?","Glycopeptides are only active against what?","Gram positive bacteria"],["","Macrolides: what are they active against?","Macrolides: what are they active against?","Gram positives; Gram negatives; Cell wall deficient bacteria"],["","How are cephalosporin generations divided?","How are cephalosporin generations divided?","By effect on Gram+ vs Gram- bacteria"]]},{"id":41036,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which cephalosporin generation is described as active against MRSA?","o":["5th (ceftaroline)","1st (cefadroxil)","2nd (cefuroxime)","3rd (ceftriaxone)","4th (cefepime)"],"e":"The cephalosporin table lists the 5th generation (ceftaroline, IV) as more active against Gram positives including MRSA, with increased β-lactamase resistance. The 3rd generation includes antipseudomonal ceftazidime. You do not need to know the table for now.","w":["1st generation is not active against MRSA.","2nd generation is more active against Gram negatives.","3rd generation is less active against Gram positives.","4th generation is not listed for MRSA."],"k":"5th-generation ceftaroline covers MRSA.","rn":[["","Which cephalosporin generation covers MRSA?","Which cephalosporin generation covers MRSA?","5th (ceftaroline)"],["","5th generation cephalosporin: route, example, spectrum?","5th generation cephalosporin: route, example, spectrum?","IV; Ceftaroline; More Gram+, including MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus); Increased beta-lactamase resistance"]]},{"id":41037,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"How does vancomycin's mechanism differ from that of β-lactams?","o":["It binds the D-Ala-D-Ala end of wall building blocks rather than the transpeptidase","It binds the transpeptidase in exactly the same way as β-lactams such as amoxicillin do","It blocks the 50S ribosome","It inhibits DNA gyrase","It is a prodrug activated by anaerobes"],"e":"Vancomycin inhibits bacterial cell wall (peptidoglycan) formation, but by binding the D-alanine-D-alanine end of the NAM/NAG building blocks rather than the transpeptidase. The building blocks cannot be joined in, the wall is weak and the bacterium dies.","w":["β-lactams bind the transpeptidase; vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala.","50S binding is macrolides.","DNA gyrase is blocked by quinolones.","The anaerobe-activated prodrug is metronidazole."],"k":"Vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala, not the PBP.","rn":[["","What does vancomycin bind instead of transpeptidase?","What does vancomycin bind instead of transpeptidase?","D-alanine-D-alanine end of precursor"],["","Cycloserine blocks which steps of murein synthesis?","Cycloserine blocks which steps of murein synthesis?","DdlB (D-Ala-D-Ala formation); Alanine racemase (L-Ala to D-Ala)"]]},{"id":41038,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"A patient has Clostridium difficile diarrhoea. Why is vancomycin given orally, even though it is poorly absorbed?","o":["Because it is not absorbed, it stays in the gut where the infection is","Oral vancomycin is absorbed into the blood better than intravenous vancomycin","It is converted to an active form by the liver","It treats the diarrhoea by binding calcium","Oral dosing prevents all side effects"],"e":"Vancomycin is very poorly absorbed from the GI tract. Given orally it is not absorbed, so it stays in the gut where Clostridium difficile is. For serious systemic Gram-positive infections it is given intravenously. This illustrates selective toxicity: a drug that stays where the infection is causes less harm elsewhere.","w":["Oral vancomycin is poorly absorbed.","It is not a liver-activated prodrug.","Calcium binding is tetracycline chelation.","Oral dosing does not prevent all side effects."],"k":"Oral vancomycin stays in the gut for C. difficile.","rn":[["","Why is oral vancomycin used for Clostridium difficile?","Why is oral vancomycin used for Clostridium difficile?","Not absorbed; Stays in the gut"]]},{"id":41039,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which adverse effects are the most important for vancomycin?","o":["Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity, as with the aminoglycosides","Orange saliva and CYP450 induction","Phototoxicity and tooth staining","Arrhythmias and nausea","Rashes from penicillin allergy"],"e":"Vancomycin is nephrotoxic (kidney damage, especially with long use) and ototoxic (hearing damage, e.g. tinnitus). Avoid giving it with other ototoxic or nephrotoxic drugs. It has a narrow therapeutic window and is dosed by blood levels.","w":["These relate to rifampicin.","These relate to tetracyclines.","These relate to clarithromycin.","Rashes relate to β-lactam allergy."],"k":"Vancomycin: nephrotoxic, ototoxic.","rn":[["","What are the 2 toxicities of aminoglycosides?","What are the 2 toxicities of aminoglycosides?","Nephrotoxicity; Ototoxicity"]]},{"id":41040,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Why is vancomycin dosed according to drug levels in the blood?","o":["It has a narrow therapeutic window","It is completely non-toxic at any dose","It is excreted in sweat","It is a CYP450 inducer","It cannot be given intravenously"],"e":"Vancomycin has a narrow therapeutic window (the range between effective and toxic levels), so it is dosed by drug levels in blood. It is excreted in urine and is nephrotoxic and ototoxic.","w":["It is nephrotoxic and ototoxic.","It is excreted in urine.","CYP450 induction is rifampicin.","It is mostly given IV."],"k":"Narrow therapeutic window → monitor vancomycin levels.","rn":[["","Vancomycin: therapeutic window?","Vancomycin: therapeutic window?","Narrow"]]},{"id":41041,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which statement about vancomycin's spectrum is correct?","o":["Only Gram positive, including many resistant strains such as MRSA","Only Gram negative organisms, including Pseudomonas wound infections","Broad spectrum, including atypicals","Only mycobacteria","Only anaerobes"],"e":"Vancomycin is active only against Gram positives, including many resistant strains such as MRSA. It is used IV for serious Gram-positive organisms that produce β-lactamases or are not responding to other treatments, and orally for C. difficile.","w":["It does not act on Gram negatives.","It is not broad spectrum.","Mycobacteria are treated with rifampicin and others.","It is not anaerobe-only; C. difficile is one Gram-positive anaerobe it treats orally."],"k":"Vancomycin: Gram positive only, including MRSA.","rn":[["","Vancomycin: activity?","Vancomycin: activity?","Only Gram positive; Many resistant strains incl. MRSA"]]},{"id":41042,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"A patient is on IV vancomycin. Another doctor proposes adding gentamicin. What is the main concern?","o":["Additive nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity","Both drugs block the same enzyme, cancelling each other","Gentamicin turns vancomycin into a prodrug","Gentamicin increases vancomycin absorption from the gut","There is no concern because they work by different mechanisms"],"e":"Vancomycin and gentamicin are both nephrotoxic and ototoxic. Giving them together adds the damage, even though they work by different mechanisms. This is a drug interaction; check the BNF when prescribing.","w":["They act by different mechanisms.","Gentamicin does not activate vancomycin.","Gentamicin does not affect vancomycin's gut absorption.","Different mechanisms do not prevent additive toxicity."],"k":"Vancomycin + gentamicin: additive kidney and ear toxicity.","rn":[["","What are the 2 toxicities of aminoglycosides?","What are the 2 toxicities of aminoglycosides?","Nephrotoxicity; Ototoxicity"],["","What are the 2 important toxicities of glycopeptides?","What are the 2 important toxicities of glycopeptides?","Nephrotoxicity; Ototoxicity"]]},{"id":41043,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","a":["vancomycin"],"s":"Name the core glycopeptide antibiotic that is nephrotoxic and ototoxic and given orally for Clostridium difficile.","e":"Vancomycin is a large glycopeptide, active only against Gram positives including MRSA, given IV for serious infections and orally for C. difficile, and is nephrotoxic and ototoxic.","k":"Vancomycin: glycopeptide core drug.","rn":[["","Vancomycin: interactions?","Vancomycin: interactions?","Other ototoxic or nephrotoxic drugs"],["","Why not give vancomycin with gentamicin?","Why not give vancomycin with gentamicin?","Both nephrotoxic and ototoxic"]]},{"id":41044,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","a":["nephrotoxicity","nephrotoxic","kidney toxicity","renal toxicity"],"s":"What term describes toxicity to the kidneys, a key adverse effect of vancomycin and gentamicin?","e":"Nephrotoxicity is toxicity to the kidneys. Vancomycin and aminoglycosides such as gentamicin are nephrotoxic and ototoxic.","k":"Nephrotoxic = kidney-toxic.","rn":[["","What are the 2 toxicities of aminoglycosides?","What are the 2 toxicities of aminoglycosides?","Nephrotoxicity; Ototoxicity"],["","What are the 2 important toxicities of glycopeptides?","What are the 2 important toxicities of glycopeptides?","Nephrotoxicity; Ototoxicity"]]},{"id":41045,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"What is the basis of selective toxicity for protein synthesis inhibitors?","o":["Bacteria have 70S ribosomes; human cells have 80S","Human cells have no ribosomes","Bacterial ribosomes are identical to human ones","Bacteria make proteins without mRNA","Human ribosomes are 70S and bacterial ribosomes are the larger 80S"],"e":"Bacteria have 70S ribosomes (50S + 30S subunits); human cells have 80S ribosomes. Drugs that jam the bacterial 70S machine stop bacteria making proteins with less effect on us.","w":["Human cells have ribosomes (80S).","They differ, which is the basis of selectivity.","Bacteria use mRNA.","This reverses the sizes."],"k":"Bacterial 70S vs human 80S ribosomes.","rn":[["","Protein synthesis inhibitors: basis of selective toxicity?","Protein synthesis inhibitors: basis of selective toxicity?","70S (bacteria) vs 80S (human) ribosomes"]]},{"id":41046,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which pairing of ribosomal subunit and drug target is correct?","o":["50S: macrolides; 30S: aminoglycosides and tetracyclines","50S: aminoglycosides such as gentamicin; 30S: macrolides such as clarithromycin","50S: quinolones; 30S: rifampicin","Both subunits: β-lactams","50S: trimethoprim; 30S: sulphonamides"],"e":"The 50S subunit (23S rRNA, 5S rRNA, >30 proteins) is the target of macrolides, ketolides, chloramphenicol, lincosamides, streptogramins, oxazolidinones and pleuromutilins. The 30S subunit (16S rRNA, 21 proteins) is the target of aminoglycosides, spectinomycin, tetracyclines and glycylcyclines.","w":["This swaps the subunits.","Quinolones target DNA gyrase; rifampicin RNA polymerase.","β-lactams target the cell wall.","Trimethoprim and sulphonamides are antimetabolites."],"k":"50S: macrolides; 30S: aminoglycosides, tetracyclines.","rn":[["","Which 3 classes target protein synthesis?","Which 3 classes target protein synthesis?","Macrolides; Aminoglycosides; Tetracyclines"],["","Which subunit do aminoglycosides and tetracyclines bind?","Which subunit do aminoglycosides and tetracyclines bind?","30S"],["","Which classes other than aminoglycosides and tetracyclines bind the 30S subunit?","Which classes other than aminoglycosides and tetracyclines bind the 30S subunit?","Spectinomycin; Glycylcyclines"]]},{"id":41047,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"In this ribosome figure, what do the letters E, P and A mark?","o":["The sites where tRNAs sit during translation","Three types of β-lactamase enzyme that bind the ribosome","The three cephalosporin generations","Three folate pathway enzymes","Three bacterial cell wall layers"],"e":"The figure labels 1 the 50S subunit and its drugs (macrolides first); 2 the 30S subunit and its drugs (aminoglycosides, tetracyclines); 3 the E, P and A sites, where tRNAs sit during translation: P (peptidyl), A (acceptor) and E (exit).","w":["β-lactamases are not shown here.","Cephalosporin generations are a separate table.","Folate enzymes are in the co-trimoxazole figure.","Cell wall layers are not shown here."],"k":"Ribosome tRNA sites: E (exit), P (peptidyl), A (acceptor).","f":"am22","rn":[["","What are the 3 tRNA sites of the 70S ribosome?","What are the 3 tRNA sites of the 70S ribosome?","P (peptidyl) site; A (acceptor) site; E (exit) site"]]},{"id":41048,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Put bacterial protein synthesis in order: (P) the empty tRNA leaves from the E site; (Q) 30S, formyl-methionyl tRNA and mRNA form an initiation complex; (R) the 50S joins to form a 70S ribosome; (S) a peptide bond joins amino acids.","o":["Q → R → S → P","R → Q → S → P","Q → S → R → P","S → Q → R → P","P → Q → R → S"],"e":"The 30S subunit, formyl-methionyl tRNA and mRNA form an initiation complex; the 50S subunit joins to make a complete 70S ribosome; the first tRNA sits in P and the next enters A; a peptide bond joins the amino acids; the ribosome moves one codon and the empty tRNA leaves from E. Different drugs block A, P or E sites or the moving step.","w":["Initiation with 30S comes first.","The 70S ribosome must form before peptide bonds.","Peptide bond formation follows assembly.","The exit step comes after peptide bond formation."],"k":"Initiation (30S) → 70S → peptide bond → translocation/exit.","rn":[["","How is a peptide bond formed on the ribosome?","How is a peptide bond formed on the ribosome?","Amino acid on P-site tRNA joins the A-site amino acid"],["","What is translocation in protein synthesis?","What is translocation in protein synthesis?","Ribosome advances one codon along mRNA; tRNAs shift sites; peptide moves on"]]},{"id":41049,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","a":["70s","70 s","70s ribosome"],"s":"What is the sedimentation size of the bacterial ribosome, made of 50S and 30S subunits?","e":"Bacterial ribosomes are 70S (50S + 30S); human ribosomes are 80S. This difference underlies the selective toxicity of protein synthesis inhibitors.","k":"Bacterial ribosome = 70S.","rn":[["","Protein synthesis inhibitors: basis of selective toxicity?","Protein synthesis inhibitors: basis of selective toxicity?","70S (bacteria) vs 80S (human) ribosomes"],["","What 2 subunits make the 70S ribosome?","What 2 subunits make the 70S ribosome?","50S; 30S"]]},{"id":41050,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"A patient with a penicillin allergy needs treatment for a streptococcal infection. Which class is a suitable alternative and why?","o":["Macrolides, because they have a similar spectrum to broad-spectrum penicillins","Carbapenems, because they are not β-lactams and so never cross-react with penicillin","Glycopeptides, because they work orally for all infections","Quinolones, because they are first line for streptococci","Aminoglycosides, because they are well absorbed orally"],"e":"Macrolides (erythromycin, clarithromycin, azithromycin) are bacteriostatic or bactericidal, active against Gram positives, Gram negatives and cell-wall-deficient bacteria, with a spectrum similar to broad-spectrum penicillins, so they can be prescribed in penicillin allergy or resistance.","w":["Carbapenems are β-lactams.","Oral vancomycin is not absorbed; it is for gut C. difficile only.","Quinolones are not given as first line for this.","Gentamicin is not active orally."],"k":"Macrolides replace penicillins in allergy.","rn":[["","Macrolides have a similar spectrum to what?","Macrolides have a similar spectrum to what?","Broad spectrum penicillins"]]},{"id":41051,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which statement about the core drug clarithromycin is correct?","o":["It inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit and is excreted as metabolites in bile","It inhibits cell wall synthesis like amoxicillin and is excreted unchanged in urine","It is poorly absorbed orally and given only IV","It is active only against Gram positives including MRSA","It turns saliva and tears orange"],"e":"Clarithromycin inhibits protein synthesis at the bacterial 50S subunit. It has a broad spectrum similar to amoxicillin (S. pyogenes, pneumococcal, coliform infections in penicillin allergy) plus cell-wall-deficient bacteria such as Mycoplasma. Oral bioavailability is good, metabolites are excreted in bile, and adverse effects are nausea, diarrhoea and possible arrhythmias.","w":["That describes amoxicillin.","Oral bioavailability is good.","Gram-positive-only activity with MRSA is vancomycin.","Orange secretions relate to rifampicin."],"k":"Clarithromycin: 50S, good oral bioavailability, bile excretion.","rn":[["","Clarithromycin: mechanism of action?","Clarithromycin: mechanism of action?","Inhibits protein synthesis; Bacterial ribosome 50S subunit"]]},{"id":41052,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"A patient taking another drug that affects heart rhythm needs a macrolide. Why is clarithromycin a concern?","o":["It may alter cardiac conduction, causing arrhythmias","It is strongly nephrotoxic, like vancomycin and gentamicin","It chelates calcium in bones","It induces CYP450","It causes penicillin allergy"],"e":"Clarithromycin's adverse effects include nausea and diarrhoea, and it may alter cardiac conduction (arrhythmias). So avoid combining it with other drugs that also affect heart rhythm.","w":["Nephrotoxicity is vancomycin and aminoglycosides.","Calcium chelation is tetracyclines.","CYP450 induction is rifampicin.","Macrolides are used in penicillin allergy."],"k":"Clarithromycin may cause arrhythmias.","rn":[["","What are the 2 adverse effects of clarithromycin?","What are the 2 adverse effects of clarithromycin?","Nausea and diarrhoea; Altered cardiac conduction (arrhythmias)"],["","Why avoid giving clarithromycin with other drugs that cause arrhythmia?","Why avoid giving clarithromycin with other drugs that cause arrhythmia?","Clarithromycin may alter cardiac conduction; Arrhythmia risk adds up"]]},{"id":41053,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which extra group of organisms does clarithromycin cover compared with amoxicillin?","o":["Cell-wall-deficient bacteria such as Mycoplasma","MRSA only","Pseudomonas only","Fungi such as Candida albicans in the mouth","Viruses such as influenza"],"e":"Clarithromycin has a broad spectrum similar to amoxicillin, plus activity against cell-wall-deficient bacteria such as Mycoplasma, which β-lactams cannot target because they act on the cell wall.","w":["MRSA needs drugs such as vancomycin.","Pseudomonas needs antipseudomonal agents.","Candida is a fungus.","Antibiotics do not treat viruses."],"k":"Clarithromycin also covers Mycoplasma.","rn":[["","Which cell wall deficient bacterium does clarithromycin treat?","Which cell wall deficient bacterium does clarithromycin treat?","Mycoplasma"]]},{"id":41054,"t":"Clinical Application","a":["clarithromycin"],"s":"Name the core macrolide antibiotic that acts on the 50S subunit and is used in penicillin allergy.","e":"Clarithromycin is the core macrolide: it inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit, has a spectrum similar to amoxicillin plus Mycoplasma, and is used in penicillin allergy.","k":"Clarithromycin: core macrolide.","rn":[["","Cellulitis in penicillin allergy: alternative?","Cellulitis in penicillin allergy: alternative?","Clarithromycin (macrolide)"]]},{"id":41055,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which statement about gentamicin is correct?","o":["It is bactericidal, not active orally, and nephrotoxic and ototoxic","It is bacteriostatic and well absorbed orally, so it is ideal for outpatient use","It has a low incidence of adverse effects","It acts on the 50S subunit","It is safe in combination with vancomycin"],"e":"Aminoglycosides such as gentamicin are bactericidal and act on the 30S subunit. Gentamicin is not active orally (poorly absorbed from the GI tract, given parenterally) and is nephrotoxic and ototoxic, so plasma levels should be monitored. Combining it with vancomycin adds toxicity.","w":["It is bactericidal and not active orally.","It has important adverse effects.","Aminoglycosides act on the 30S subunit.","The combination adds nephro- and ototoxicity."],"k":"Gentamicin: bactericidal, parenteral, nephro/ototoxic.","rn":[["","Is gentamicin active when given orally?","Is gentamicin active when given orally?","No; Poorly absorbed; given parenterally"],["","Why not give vancomycin with gentamicin?","Why not give vancomycin with gentamicin?","Both nephrotoxic and ototoxic"],["","Why must gentamicin levels be monitored?","Why must gentamicin levels be monitored?","Nephrotoxic and ototoxic; Plasma levels are checked"]]},{"id":41056,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"A 6-year-old needs an antibiotic. Why should doxycycline be avoided?","o":["Tetracyclines chelate calcium, staining teeth and inhibiting bone growth","Doxycycline is nephrotoxic in children only, so it damages their developing kidneys","Doxycycline causes orange urine","Doxycycline is a β-lactam and causes allergy","Doxycycline cannot cross into the blood"],"e":"Tetracyclines (doxycycline, minocycline) are bacteriostatic and broad spectrum. They chelate metal ions such as calcium, so they are deposited in teeth and bone: tooth discolouration and inhibited bone growth. They are not given to children under 9, and the T/F answers add they should be avoided in pregnancy. They are also phototoxic.","w":["Nephrotoxicity is not the listed tetracycline effect.","Orange secretions are rifampicin.","Tetracyclines are not β-lactams.","Absorption is not the reason given."],"k":"Tetracyclines: avoid under 9 and in pregnancy (teeth, bone).","f":"am25","rn":[["","Image card: Which antibiotic class causes this? What are the 2 effects?","Which antibiotic class causes this? What are the 2 effects?","Tetracyclines; Deposition in teeth; Bone growth inhibition"],["","Why is tetracycline avoided under 9 years old?","Why is tetracycline avoided under 9 years old?","Binds calcium (chelation); Growing teeth and bones need calcium"]]},{"id":41057,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which pair of adverse effects is listed for tetracyclines?","o":["Phototoxicity and chelation of metal ions","Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity","Arrhythmias and nausea","Orange secretions and CYP450 induction","Mutagenicity and teratogenicity"],"e":"Tetracyclines are phototoxic (toxic effect triggered by light) and chelate metal ions, leading to deposition in teeth and bone growth inhibition. Aminoglycosides are nephro- and ototoxic; clarithromycin causes arrhythmias; rifampicin causes orange secretions and CYP450 induction; metronidazole is considered mutagenic and teratogenic.","w":["These are aminoglycoside effects.","These are clarithromycin effects.","These are rifampicin effects.","These are metronidazole concerns."],"k":"Tetracyclines: phototoxic, chelate metal ions.","rn":[["","What are the 2 adverse effects of tetracyclines?","What are the 2 adverse effects of tetracyclines?","Phototoxicity; Chelation of metal ions"]]},{"id":41058,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","a":["chelation","chelate","chelating"],"s":"What term describes the binding of tetracyclines to metal ions such as calcium?","e":"Chelation is the binding of ions or molecules to metal ions. Tetracyclines chelate calcium, so they deposit in developing teeth and bone.","k":"Chelation = binding to metal ions.","rn":[["","What is meant by Chelation?","What is meant by Chelation?","Binding of ions or molecules to metal ions"],["","What are the 2 adverse effects of tetracyclines?","What are the 2 adverse effects of tetracyclines?","Phototoxicity; Chelation of metal ions"]]},{"id":41059,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","a":["phototoxicity","phototoxic"],"s":"What term describes a toxic skin effect triggered by exposure to light, seen with tetracyclines?","e":"Phototoxicity is a toxic effect triggered by exposure to light (chemically induced skin irritation), an adverse effect of tetracyclines.","k":"Phototoxic = light-triggered toxicity.","rn":[["","What is meant by Phototoxicity?","What is meant by Phototoxicity?","Toxic effect triggered by light; Chemically induced skin irritation"]]},{"id":41060,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which statement about aminoglycosides compared with tetracyclines is correct?","o":["Aminoglycosides are bactericidal; tetracyclines are bacteriostatic","Aminoglycosides are bacteriostatic; tetracyclines are bactericidal","Both are bacteriostatic","Both act on the 50S subunit","Both are well absorbed orally and non-toxic"],"e":"Both act on the 30S subunit. Aminoglycosides (gentamicin) are bactericidal, with nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity. Tetracyclines (doxycycline, minocycline) are bacteriostatic and broad spectrum, with phototoxicity and metal ion chelation.","w":["This reverses them.","Aminoglycosides are bactericidal.","Both act on 30S.","Gentamicin is not active orally and both have adverse effects."],"k":"Aminoglycosides bactericidal; tetracyclines bacteriostatic.","rn":[["","Aminoglycosides: bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Aminoglycosides: bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Bactericidal"],["","Tetracyclines: bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Tetracyclines: bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Bacteriostatic"]]},{"id":41061,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"An exam asks: \"quinolones block which enzyme?\" Which answer is correct?","o":["DNA gyrase","Transpeptidase (penicillin-binding protein)","RNA polymerase in the bacterial cell","Dihydrofolate reductase in the folate pathway","β-lactamase made by resistant bacteria"],"e":"Quinolones (ciprofloxacin, nalidixic acid, norfloxacin, ofloxacin) inhibit DNA gyrases, which are needed for supercoiling, replication and separation of circular bacterial DNA, causing rapid bacterial death. DNA gyrase is a topoisomerase catalysing ATP-dependent negative supercoiling.","w":["Transpeptidase is blocked by β-lactams.","RNA polymerase is blocked by rifampicin.","Dihydrofolate reductase is blocked by trimethoprim.","β-lactamase is inhibited by clavulanic acid."],"k":"Quinolones inhibit DNA gyrase.","rn":[["","What is DNA gyrase needed for?","What is DNA gyrase needed for?","Supercoiling; Replication; Separation of circular DNA"],["","Which enzyme do quinolones block?","Which enzyme do quinolones block?","DNA gyrase"],["","What is DNA gyrase?","What is DNA gyrase?","Essential, mainly bacterial enzyme; Catalyses ATP-dependent negative supercoiling of closed circular DNA; A topoisomerase"]]},{"id":41062,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"Which statement about quinolones is consistent with the lecture?","o":["They are used for pseudomonal and GI infections and need caution in children","They are always the first-line choice for every uncomplicated UTI in the community","They are active only against Gram positives","They have no drug interactions","They inhibit the 50S ribosome"],"e":"Quinolones have a spectrum that is mostly Gram negative, also Gram positive and anaerobes. They are used for UTIs (not first line), pseudomonal infection, GI infections, prostatitis and sexually transmitted infections. They have several interactions, need caution in children, and may lead to resistant bacteria.","w":["They are not first line for UTIs.","They are mostly Gram-negative in spectrum.","They have several interactions.","They act on DNA gyrase."],"k":"Quinolones: mostly Gram −; caution in children; interactions.","rn":[["","What 5 infections are quinolones used for?","What 5 infections are quinolones used for?","UTIs (not first line); Pseudomonal infection; GI infections; Prostatitis; STIs"],["","What are the important cautions with quinolones?","What are the important cautions with quinolones?","Several interactions; Caution in children; Can lead to resistant bacteria"]]},{"id":41063,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","a":["dna gyrase","gyrase","topoisomerase","dna gyrases"],"s":"Name the bacterial topoisomerase that catalyses ATP-dependent negative supercoiling of DNA and is blocked by quinolones.","e":"DNA gyrase is an essential, mainly bacterial topoisomerase catalysing ATP-dependent negative supercoiling of closed-circular double-stranded DNA. Quinolones block it, so DNA cannot be copied and the bacterium dies.","k":"DNA gyrase: target of quinolones.","rn":[["","What is DNA gyrase?","What is DNA gyrase?","Essential, mainly bacterial enzyme; Catalyses ATP-dependent negative supercoiling of closed circular DNA; A topoisomerase"]]},{"id":41064,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"Why is metronidazole effective mainly against anaerobic organisms?","o":["It is a prodrug that only anaerobes can activate","It binds the D-Ala-D-Ala tails of the anaerobic bacterial wall","It blocks the 30S subunit of anaerobic ribosomes only","It inhibits folate synthesis in anaerobes only","It is excreted in saliva, where anaerobes live"],"e":"Metronidazole (a nitroimidazole) is a prodrug: only anaerobic organisms can metabolise it to its active form, and the metabolites are toxic to DNA. It is bactericidal.","w":["D-Ala-D-Ala binding is vancomycin.","30S inhibition is aminoglycosides and tetracyclines.","Folate inhibition is antimetabolites.","Excretion route is not the reason."],"k":"Metronidazole: prodrug activated by anaerobes, toxic to DNA.","rn":[["","Why does metronidazole only work on anaerobes?","Why does metronidazole only work on anaerobes?","Prodrug; Only anaerobes activate it"]]},{"id":41065,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"A pregnant woman has an anaerobic infection. Why does the lecturer advise against metronidazole?","o":["It is considered potentially teratogenic","It turns urine orange","It is nephrotoxic","It stains the fetus's teeth","It is a strong CYP450 inducer that lowers levels of pregnancy hormones"],"e":"Metronidazole is considered potentially mutagenic, carcinogenic and teratogenic (may produce physical or functional defects in the embryo or foetus). Because of its effects on the embryo and foetus, the lecturer says it should not be prescribed in pregnancy.","w":["Orange secretions relate to rifampicin.","Nephrotoxicity relates to vancomycin and aminoglycosides.","Tooth staining relates to tetracyclines.","CYP450 induction relates to rifampicin."],"k":"Metronidazole: potentially teratogenic; avoid in pregnancy.","rn":[["","Metronidazole is considered potentially what 3 things?","Metronidazole is considered potentially what 3 things?","Mutagenic; Carcinogenic; Teratogenic"]]},{"id":41066,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","a":["teratogenic","teratogen"],"s":"What term describes a drug that may produce physical or functional defects in the embryo or foetus?","e":"Teratogenic means able to produce physical or functional defects in the embryo or foetus. Metronidazole is considered potentially mutagenic, carcinogenic and teratogenic.","k":"Teratogenic = causes foetal defects.","rn":[["","What is meant by Teratogenic?","What is meant by Teratogenic?","Physical or functional defects in embryo or foetus"]]},{"id":41067,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"A patient on TB treatment panics because his saliva, sweat and tears are orange. Which drug explains this?","o":["Rifampicin","Metronidazole","Vancomycin","Clarithromycin","Doxycycline"],"e":"Rifampicin binds RNA polymerase, inhibiting mRNA synthesis, and is bactericidal against mycobacteria (M. tuberculosis, M. leprae). It causes orange colouring of saliva, tears and sweat (and urine may look red): it isn't blood, the drug is excreted in these fluids. It is also a strong CYP450 inducer.","w":["Metronidazole does not cause orange secretions.","Vancomycin is nephro/ototoxic.","Clarithromycin may cause arrhythmias.","Doxycycline stains teeth."],"k":"Rifampicin: orange secretions.","rn":[["","Which body fluids does rifampicin turn orange?","Which body fluids does rifampicin turn orange?","Saliva; Tears; Sweat"]]},{"id":41068,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"Why must doctors check other medicines when starting rifampicin?","o":["It strongly induces CYP450, speeding the breakdown of other drugs","It inhibits all of the liver's metabolising enzymes, so it raises other drug levels","It binds calcium and blocks absorption of other drugs","It is nephrotoxic with other drugs","It has no interactions"],"e":"Rifampicin is a strong inducer of CYP450 (cytochrome P450), causing metabolic interactions: it speeds up the breakdown of other drugs, as in the previous pharmacology lecture.","w":["It induces, not inhibits, CYP450.","Calcium binding is tetracyclines.","Nephrotoxicity is vancomycin and aminoglycosides.","It has important interactions."],"k":"Rifampicin induces CYP450."},{"id":41069,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"What is rifampicin's mechanism of action?","o":["It binds RNA polymerase, inhibiting mRNA synthesis","It binds DNA gyrase and stops supercoiling of the bacterial chromosome","It binds the 30S subunit","It blocks dihydropteroate synthetase","It binds penicillin-binding proteins"],"e":"Rifampicin binds RNA polymerase, inhibiting mRNA synthesis, and is bactericidal against mycobacteria such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Mycobacterium leprae. It is an important anti-TB drug.","w":["DNA gyrase is targeted by quinolones.","30S is targeted by aminoglycosides and tetracyclines.","Dihydropteroate synthetase is blocked by sulfonamides.","PBPs are targeted by β-lactams."],"k":"Rifampicin inhibits RNA polymerase.","rn":[["","Rifampicin: mechanism of action?","Rifampicin: mechanism of action?","Binds RNA polymerase; Inhibits mRNA synthesis"]]},{"id":41070,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","a":["rifampicin","rifampin"],"s":"Name the anti-TB drug that inhibits RNA polymerase and turns saliva and tears orange.","e":"Rifampicin binds bacterial RNA polymerase, is bactericidal against mycobacteria, induces CYP450 and causes orange saliva, tears and sweat.","k":"Rifampicin: anti-TB, RNA polymerase, orange fluids.","rn":[["","Which body fluids does rifampicin turn orange?","Which body fluids does rifampicin turn orange?","Saliva; Tears; Sweat"]]},{"id":41071,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"Why is co-trimoxazole described as synergistic?","o":["Its two drugs block different stages of the same tetrahydrofolate pathway","Its two drugs both block the same single enzyme, giving a double dose at one step","It contains a β-lactamase inhibitor","It acts on both 50S and 30S subunits","It is a prodrug activated by anaerobes"],"e":"Co-trimoxazole is a sulfonamide plus trimethoprim, a synergistic association: the combined effect is greater than the sum of the parts. Both act in the same pathway (synthesis of tetrahydrofolate) at different stages. Alone each is bacteriostatic; co-trimoxazole may be bactericidal, and resistance is not common.","w":["They block different enzymes.","Clavulanic acid is the β-lactamase inhibitor in co-amoxiclav.","Antimetabolites do not act on ribosomes.","The anaerobe-activated prodrug is metronidazole."],"k":"Co-trimoxazole: two blocks in the folate pathway → synergy.","rn":[["","What pathway do sulfonamide and trimethoprim block?","What pathway do sulfonamide and trimethoprim block?","Tetrahydrofolate synthesis; At different stages"]]},{"id":41072,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"In the folate pathway, which enzyme does trimethoprim block?","o":["Dihydrofolate reductase","Dihydropteroate synthetase","DNA gyrase","RNA polymerase","Transpeptidase"],"e":"PABA is converted by dihydropteroate synthetase (blocked by sulfonamides) towards folate; dihydrofolate reductase makes tetrahydrofolate (blocked by trimethoprim); tetrahydrofolate is needed for thymidylate and so DNA synthesis. You do not need the enzyme names for FHD.","w":["Dihydropteroate synthetase is blocked by sulfonamides.","DNA gyrase is blocked by quinolones.","RNA polymerase is blocked by rifampicin.","Transpeptidase is blocked by β-lactams."],"k":"Trimethoprim blocks dihydrofolate reductase.","rn":[["","Which enzyme does trimethoprim block?","Which enzyme does trimethoprim block?","Dihydrofolate reductase"]]},{"id":41073,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"In this folate pathway figure, which drug class blocks the first enzyme after PABA?","o":["Sulfonamides","Trimethoprim","Quinolones","Macrolides","β-lactams"],"e":"The figure runs from PABA down to DNA. Sulfonamides block the first enzyme (dihydropteroate synthetase); trimethoprim blocks dihydrofolate reductase. Blocking two steps of the same pathway is why the pair is synergistic.","w":["Trimethoprim blocks the later step.","Quinolones block DNA gyrase.","Macrolides block the 50S ribosome.","β-lactams block cell wall synthesis."],"k":"Sulfonamides block the first folate step.","f":"am29","rn":[["","Which enzyme do sulfonamides block?","Which enzyme do sulfonamides block?","Dihydropteroate synthetase (PABA step)"]]},{"id":41074,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"Which statement about trimethoprim from the true/false exercise is correct?","o":["It can result in folate deficiency because it is an antimetabolite","It is a cell wall inhibitor that binds penicillin-binding proteins","It is bactericidal on its own in all cases","It induces CYP450","It cannot be used for UTIs"],"e":"Trimethoprim can result in folate deficiency: it inhibits folate's role in nucleotide synthesis (an antimetabolite). Beyond the learning outcomes, folate deficiency can cause megaloblastic anaemia, prevented in long-term treatment by giving folinic acid. Alone it is bacteriostatic.","w":["It is an antimetabolite.","Alone it is bacteriostatic.","CYP450 induction is rifampicin.","It was used empirically for UTI in the lab lecture case."],"k":"Trimethoprim can cause folate deficiency.","rn":[["","Why does trimethoprim cause folate deficiency?","Why does trimethoprim cause folate deficiency?","Inhibits the role of folate in nucleotide synthesis"],["","Trimethoprim can cause what deficiency?","Trimethoprim can cause what deficiency?","Folate deficiency"],["","What can folate deficiency cause?","What can folate deficiency cause?","Megaloblastic anaemia"]]},{"id":41075,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","a":["synergistic","synergy","synergism"],"s":"What term describes a drug combination whose effect is greater than the sum of its parts?","e":"Synergistic: the combined effect is greater than the sum of the parts, as with co-trimoxazole (sulfonamide + trimethoprim).","k":"Synergistic = more than additive.","rn":[["","What is meant by Synergistic Association?","What is meant by Synergistic Association?","Combined effect greater than sum of separate effects"]]},{"id":41076,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","a":["antimetabolite","antimetabolites"],"s":"What term describes drugs chemically similar to natural metabolites that interfere with normal metabolic pathways?","e":"Antimetabolites are drugs chemically similar to natural metabolites but different enough to interfere with normal metabolic pathways, such as trimethoprim and sulphonamides in folate synthesis.","k":"Antimetabolites mimic metabolites to block pathways.","rn":[["","What is meant by Antimetabolite?","What is meant by Antimetabolite?","Chemically similar to a natural metabolite; Interferes with normal metabolic pathways"]]},{"id":41077,"t":"Mechanisms of Action","s":"Which statement about antibiotic resistance is consistent with the lecture?","o":["Resistance has followed each new antibiotic class within years","Resistance appeared only after 2000","Resistance affects only infections acquired in hospital, not common community infections","No new resistance has appeared since penicillin","Resistance improves patient outcomes"],"e":"Resistance has followed each new class within years: e.g. penicillin discovered 1928, resistance 1941; sulfonamides 1932, resistance 1942. It affects common and hospital-acquired infections (MRSA, multidrug-resistant Gram negatives), and patients with drug-resistant infections are at increased risk of worse outcomes and death.","w":["Resistance appeared soon after early discoveries.","It affects common infections too.","Resistance keeps appearing.","It worsens outcomes."],"k":"Resistance follows every new antibiotic class.","rn":[["","Within each antibiotic class, what 2 distinctions apply?","Within each antibiotic class, what 2 distinctions apply?","Bacteriostatic vs bactericidal; Narrow vs broad spectrum"]]},{"id":41078,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"On this timeline, how long after penicillin's discovery (1928) did resistance appear?","o":["About 13 years (1941)","About 1 year (1929)","About 50 years (1978)","About 80 years (2008)","Resistance never appeared"],"e":"The timeline shows penicillin discovered in 1928 and resistance appearing in 1941. Sulfonamides were discovered in 1932 with resistance by 1942; resistance follows each discovery within years.","w":["Resistance did not appear within a year.","It appeared much sooner than 50 years.","It appeared much sooner than 80 years.","Resistance did appear."],"k":"Penicillin 1928 → resistance 1941.","f":"am31","rn":[["","Penicillin: year discovered vs resistance seen","Penicillin: year discovered vs resistance seen","Discovered 1928; Resistance 1941"]]},{"id":41079,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which example of antibiotic misuse did the lecturer give?","o":["A 7-day course prescribed, but only 5 days taken","A drug chosen after culture and sensitivity results","Checking interactions in the BNF before prescribing","Asking about penicillin allergy","Reducing the dose in liver failure"],"e":"The lecturer's examples of misuse: a course not finished (7 days prescribed, only 5 taken); a drug not targeting that bacterium; an ineffective dose (needs 1000 mg, given 500 mg). The slide words misuse as treatment too long, too broad or ineffective.","w":["Using culture results is good practice.","Checking interactions is good practice.","Asking about allergy is good practice.","Dose reduction in liver failure is good practice."],"k":"Misuse: incomplete course, wrong drug, too low a dose.","rn":[["","Penicillin V: route and drawback?","Penicillin V: route and drawback?","Oral; Taken several times a day"]]},{"id":41080,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"How do bacteria change to become resistant, according to the lecture?","o":["By mutation and gene transfer","By forming a nucleus and becoming eukaryotic cells","By becoming viruses","By turning into fungi","By losing all their DNA"],"e":"Bacteria change their structure by mutation and gene transfer, which the lecturer compared to new COVID-19 variants. Resistance may affect the recipient and may also spread clonally or by gene transfer.","w":["Bacteria remain prokaryotes without a nucleus.","Bacteria do not become viruses.","Bacteria do not become fungi.","They keep their DNA."],"k":"Resistance arises by mutation and gene transfer.","rn":[["","How can drug resistance spread beyond the recipient?","How can drug resistance spread beyond the recipient?","Clonally; By gene transfer"]]},{"id":41081,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"About how many new cases of multidrug-resistant TB (MDR-TB) were reported in 2013?","o":["About 480,000","About 4,800","About 48","About 48 million","None"],"e":"The lecture states that in 2013 there were about 480,000 new cases of multidrug-resistant tuberculosis (MDR-TB). Patients with drug-resistant infections are at increased risk of worse outcomes and death.","w":["This is far too low.","This is far too low.","This is far too high.","Many cases were reported."],"k":"MDR-TB 2013: ~480,000 new cases.","rn":[["","How many new cases of MDR-TB (multidrug-resistant tuberculosis) were there in 2013?","How many new cases of MDR-TB (multidrug-resistant tuberculosis) were there in 2013?","About 480 000"]]},{"id":41082,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a mechanism of antibiotic resistance?","o":["Increased host antibody production","Inactivation or modification of the antibiotic","Alteration of the target","Reduced uptake of the antibiotic","Enhanced export by efflux pumps"],"e":"Resistance mechanisms: inactivation or modification of the antibiotic (e.g. β-lactamases); alteration of microbial enzymes that transform pro-drugs to effective moieties; alteration of the target; reduced uptake; enhanced export (efflux pumps); and development of alternative pathways. Host antibodies are not a bacterial resistance mechanism.","w":["Inactivation is listed.","Target alteration is listed.","Reduced uptake is listed.","Efflux pumps are listed."],"k":"Six mechanisms: destroy, no activation, change target, keep out, pump out, bypass."},{"id":41083,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"An anaerobe stops converting metronidazole into its active toxic form. Which resistance mechanism is this?","o":["Alteration of microbial enzymes that transform pro-drugs","Enhanced export by efflux pumps","Hydrolysis of the drug by a β-lactamase enzyme","Development of alternative pathways","Alteration of the ribosomal target"],"e":"One mechanism is alteration of microbial enzymes that transform pro-drugs to their effective moieties. Metronidazole is a prodrug that anaerobes must activate, so altering that activation makes the organism resistant.","w":["Efflux pumps export active drug.","β-lactamase hydrolyses β-lactams.","Alternative pathways bypass a blocked step.","Ribosomal changes affect protein synthesis inhibitors."],"k":"Prodrug resistance: altered activating enzymes.","rn":[["","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on the drug or target?","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on the drug or target?","Inactivate or modify antibiotic; Alter pro-drug activating enzymes; Alter the target"]]},{"id":41084,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"Mutations in bacterial ribosomes reduce the binding of several drugs. Which statement fits?","o":["This is alteration of the target, affecting protein synthesis inhibitors","This is β-lactamase production","This affects only β-lactams such as amoxicillin and penicillin","This is a host factor","This cannot cause resistance"],"e":"The T/F answers state resistance may be due to mutations in bacterial ribosomes, which can reduce binding of many antibiotics that inhibit protein synthesis. This is an example of alteration of the target.","w":["β-lactamase inactivates β-lactams.","β-lactams target the cell wall, not ribosomes.","It is a bacterial change.","It is stated to cause resistance."],"k":"Ribosomal mutation = target alteration.","rn":[["","How do bacterial ribosome mutations cause resistance?","How do bacterial ribosome mutations cause resistance?","Reduce binding of protein synthesis inhibitors"]]},{"id":41085,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","a":["efflux pump","efflux pumps","efflux"],"s":"What is the name for bacterial pumps that actively export an antibiotic out of the cell?","e":"Efflux pumps export antibiotics, one of the listed resistance mechanisms (enhanced export).","k":"Efflux pumps export antibiotics.","rn":[["","What is meant by Efflux Pump?","What is meant by Efflux Pump?","Pumps the antibiotic out of the cell"],["","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on entry or pathways?","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on entry or pathways?","Reduced uptake; Enhanced export (efflux pumps); Alternative pathways"]]},{"id":41086,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"What does VRE stand for, and why is it a problem?","o":["Vancomycin-resistant enterococcus: the former drug of choice no longer works","Viral respiratory enteritis: a virus resistant to all drugs","Very resistant E. coli: an extended-spectrum β-lactamase producer in the gut","Vancomycin-related enterotoxin: a toxin from vancomycin","Variable resistance in enterobacteria: harmless"],"e":"VRE is vancomycin-resistant enterococcus: vancomycin used to be the drug of choice for enterococcus but no longer works. Other problematic resistance: MRSA, ESBL/AMPC producers, TB and Acinetobacter.","w":["VRE is a bacterium, not a virus.","ESBL is a separate group.","Vancomycin does not make an enterotoxin.","It is a significant problem."],"k":"VRE: vancomycin no longer works on enterococcus.","rn":[["","What is VRE resistant to, and why does it matter?","What is VRE resistant to, and why does it matter?","Vancomycin; Vancomycin was the drug of choice for enterococcus"]]},{"id":41087,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"Which is NOT listed among the problematic resistant organisms?","o":["Lactobacillus casei","MRSA","VRE","ESBL / AMPC producing organisms","Acinetobacter"],"e":"Problematic resistance listed: MRSA, VRE, ESBL/AMPC (extended-spectrum β-lactamase) producing organisms, TB and Acinetobacter.","w":["MRSA is listed.","VRE is listed.","ESBL/AMPC producers are listed.","Acinetobacter is listed."],"k":"Problem organisms: MRSA, VRE, ESBL/AMPC, TB, Acinetobacter."},{"id":41088,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"Which strategy is listed for fighting the spread of resistance?","o":["Track resistant bacteria and improve antibiotic use","Prescribe broad-spectrum antibiotics for every infection","Give antibiotics for viral flu to prevent bacteria","Prescribe over the phone whenever asked","Stop developing new antibiotics"],"e":"Fighting the spread: prevent infections and the spread of resistant bacteria; track resistant bacteria; improve the use of antibiotics; promote development of new antibiotics and new diagnostic tests; follow NICE guidance. NHS advice: only prescribe when absolutely necessary and not over the telephone.","w":["Broad spectrum increases resistance.","Antibiotics are not for flu.","Phone prescribing on request is discouraged.","New antibiotics should be promoted."],"k":"Fight resistance: prevent spread, track, prescribe wisely, develop new drugs.","rn":[["","What are the 5 ways to fight antibiotic resistance?","What are the 5 ways to fight antibiotic resistance?","Prevent infections and spread; Track resistant bacteria; Improve antibiotic use; New antibiotics and diagnostic tests; NICE guidance"]]},{"id":41089,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","a":["mrsa","meticillin-resistant staphylococcus aureus","methicillin-resistant staphylococcus aureus","methicillin resistant staphylococcus aureus"],"s":"What resistant organism's name means that meticillin no longer works on this Staphylococcus aureus?","e":"MRSA is meticillin (methicillin)-resistant Staphylococcus aureus: meticillin no longer works on these S. aureus. Vancomycin and 5th-generation cephalosporins (ceftaroline) are active against MRSA.","k":"MRSA = meticillin-resistant S. aureus.","rn":[["","MRSA","MRSA","Meticillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus"]]},{"id":41090,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","a":["esbl","extended-spectrum beta-lactamase","extended spectrum beta lactamase","extended-spectrum β-lactamase"],"s":"What does the abbreviation ESBL stand for?","e":"ESBL stands for extended-spectrum β-lactamase; ESBL/AMPC producing organisms are among the problematic resistant bacteria.","k":"ESBL = extended-spectrum β-lactamase.","rn":[["","ESBL","ESBL","Extended spectrum beta-lactamase"]]},{"id":41091,"t":"Clinical Application","s":"An 80-year-old with chronic leg oedema from heart failure develops cellulitis after a chiropody visit. What is the most common pathogen, and what is its usual susceptibility?","o":["Streptococcus pyogenes, always susceptible to penicillins","MRSA, resistant to all β-lactams","Pseudomonas, susceptible only to piperacillin","Mycobacterium tuberculosis, susceptible to rifampicin","Clostridium difficile, susceptible to oral vancomycin"],"e":"The most common pathogen in cellulitis is Streptococcus pyogenes, which is always susceptible to penicillins. Choosing antibiotics requires knowing which bacteria cause which clinical syndromes and bacterial susceptibility to different antibiotics.","w":["MRSA is not given as the most common cause of cellulitis.","Pseudomonas is not the most common cause.","TB is not a cause of cellulitis here.","C. difficile causes diarrhoea."],"k":"Cellulitis: usually S. pyogenes, penicillin-susceptible.","rn":[["","Is Streptococcus pyogenes susceptible to penicillins?","Is Streptococcus pyogenes susceptible to penicillins?","Yes, always"]]},{"id":41092,"t":"Clinical Application","s":"The same patient is allergic to penicillin. Which antibiotic is the recommended alternative?","o":["Clarithromycin, a macrolide with a similar spectrum","Amoxicillin, at a lower dose to avoid allergy","Co-amoxiclav, because clavulanic acid prevents allergy","Flucloxacillin, a β-lactamase-resistant penicillin","Metronidazole, because it covers all streptococci"],"e":"Because she is allergic to penicillin, the alternative is a macrolide (clarithromycin), which has a similar spectrum. Three days later culture confirmed the strep was sensitive to clarithromycin and the leg improved.","w":["Amoxicillin is a penicillin and would risk allergy.","Co-amoxiclav still contains amoxicillin.","Flucloxacillin is a penicillin.","Metronidazole targets anaerobes."],"k":"Penicillin allergy → clarithromycin.","rn":[["","Cellulitis in penicillin allergy: alternative?","Cellulitis in penicillin allergy: alternative?","Clarithromycin (macrolide)"]]},{"id":41093,"t":"Clinical Application","s":"Three days into clarithromycin, the cellulitis is better but she has a sore mouth with white plaques. What is the best explanation?","o":["Antibiotics selected out commensals, letting Candida cause oral thrush","The streptococcus causing the cellulitis has spread through the blood to the mouth","Clarithromycin has caused penicillin allergy","The mouth sores are orange rifampicin deposits","Clarithromycin has stained her teeth"],"e":"Antibiotics select out commensals, letting Candida cause symptoms: oral thrush. Candida albicans was probably a commensal in this patient, but antibiotic therapy made it a pathogen.","w":["The cellulitis improved and the plaques are fungal.","Clarithromycin does not cause penicillin allergy.","She is not taking rifampicin.","Tooth staining is a tetracycline effect."],"k":"Antibiotics → loss of commensals → Candida thrush.","f":"am42","rn":[["","Image card: What is this? Why can it follow antibiotics?","What is this? Why can it follow antibiotics?","Oral thrush (Candida albicans); Antibiotics select out commensals"]]},{"id":41094,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"Which take-home message from the cellulitis case is correct?","o":["Allergy to penicillins is common, so alternatives must be known","Penicillin allergy is extremely rare, so it does not need to be asked about before prescribing","Antibiotics never affect commensal organisms","Choosing antibiotics does not require knowledge of syndromes","Candida is always a pathogen"],"e":"The case's key points: choosing antibiotics requires knowing which bacteria cause which syndromes and their susceptibilities; allergy to penicillins is common; Candida albicans was probably a commensal, but antibiotics made it a pathogen.","w":["Penicillin allergy is common.","Antibiotics affect commensals.","Syndrome knowledge is essential.","Candida is often a commensal."],"k":"Penicillin allergy is common; antibiotics can make commensals pathogens.","rn":[["","Why must a prescriber ask about allergy before prescribing an antibiotic?","Why must a prescriber ask about allergy before prescribing an antibiotic?","Allergy to penicillins is common; A reaction can happen suddenly"]]},{"id":41095,"t":"Clinical Application","s":"Which statement about broad-spectrum penicillins is correct?","o":["They may alter gut flora, allowing overgrowth of pathogens such as C. difficile","They never disturb the normal gut flora, even with long courses","They kill bacteria by binding ribosomes","They cannot be given to anyone with any allergy","They are only given intravenously"],"e":"The true/false answers: broad-spectrum penicillins may alter gut flora, allowing overgrowth of pathogens such as Clostridium difficile, so \"do not disturb gut flora\" is false. Penicillins disrupt cell wall synthesis, not ribosomes.","w":["They can disturb gut flora.","Penicillins act on the cell wall.","The key concern is penicillin allergy specifically.","Many are given orally (e.g. amoxicillin)."],"k":"Broad-spectrum penicillins can disturb gut flora (C. difficile).","rn":[["","Do broad spectrum penicillins disturb gut flora?","Do broad spectrum penicillins disturb gut flora?","Yes; Can allow C. difficile overgrowth"]]},{"id":41096,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Can a patient allergic to penicillin be given a cephalosporin?","o":["Yes, with caution, as a minority are also allergic to cephalosporins","Never, under any circumstances, because cross-reaction is certain","Yes, with no caution at all","Only if given orally","Only with clavulanic acid"],"e":"The true/false answer: \"individuals allergic to penicillin cannot be given cephalosporins\" is false. They can be given cephalosporins with caution, because a minority are also allergic to cephalosporins.","w":["It is not absolutely forbidden.","Caution is needed.","Route does not remove the risk.","Clavulanic acid is a β-lactamase inhibitor, not an allergy protector."],"k":"Penicillin allergy: cephalosporins with caution.","rn":[["","Can penicillin-allergic patients have cephalosporins?","Can penicillin-allergic patients have cephalosporins?","Yes, with caution; A minority are also allergic"]]},{"id":41097,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which statement about macrolides is correct?","o":["They can cause GI disturbance, especially erythromycin","They never cause any gastrointestinal side effects because they act only on bacteria","They are nephrotoxic and ototoxic","They stain teeth in children","They inhibit DNA gyrase"],"e":"The true/false answer: macrolides can cause GI disturbances because they are broad spectrum, especially erythromycin; clarithromycin is better tolerated. Clarithromycin's adverse effects include nausea, diarrhoea and possible arrhythmias.","w":["GI disturbance is a recognised effect.","Nephro/ototoxicity relates to vancomycin and aminoglycosides.","Tooth staining relates to tetracyclines.","DNA gyrase is targeted by quinolones."],"k":"Macrolides cause GI upset (erythromycin worst).","rn":[["","Which macrolide is better tolerated: erythromycin or clarithromycin?","Which macrolide is better tolerated: erythromycin or clarithromycin?","Clarithromycin; Erythromycin causes more GI disturbance"]]},{"id":41098,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which statement about gentamicin from the true/false exercise is correct?","o":["It is not active orally and is given parenterally","It has a low incidence of adverse effects, so plasma levels need no monitoring","It is bacteriostatic","It is the first-line drug for TB","It acts on the cell wall"],"e":"The true/false answers: gentamicin is not active orally (poorly absorbed from the GI tract, given parenterally) — true; \"gentamicin has a low incidence of adverse effects\" is false: it is nephrotoxic and ototoxic, and plasma levels should be monitored.","w":["It has significant adverse effects.","Aminoglycosides are bactericidal.","Rifampicin is the key TB drug named.","It acts on the 30S ribosome."],"k":"Gentamicin: parenteral; nephro/ototoxic; monitor levels.","rn":[["","Is gentamicin active when given orally?","Is gentamicin active when given orally?","No; Poorly absorbed; given parenterally"]]},{"id":41099,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which statement about tetracyclines is TRUE?","o":["They should be avoided in pregnancy and in young children","They are safe in all children because they are bacteriostatic","They are only given intravenously","They are β-lactams","They improve tooth enamel"],"e":"The true/false answer: tetracyclines should be avoided during pregnancy and for young children because they chelate calcium and form permanent yellow-brown deposits on developing teeth. They are not given to children under 9.","w":["Being bacteriostatic does not make them safe in children.","Doxycycline is used orally.","They are not β-lactams.","They stain teeth."],"k":"Tetracyclines: avoid in pregnancy and under 9.","rn":[["","Who should avoid tetracyclines?","Who should avoid tetracyclines?","Pregnant women; Young children (under 9)"]]},{"id":41100,"t":"Antibiotics","s":"Who coined the term \"chemotherapy\" for synthetic chemicals that destroy infectious agents?","o":["Ehrlich","Alexander Fleming","Peter Piot","Robert Koch","Louis Pasteur"],"e":"Ehrlich coined the term chemotherapy: the use of synthetic chemicals capable of destroying infectious agents. Chemotherapy originally meant chemical treatment to kill germs, not cancer drugs. Alexander Fleming discovered penicillin by chance while working with a fungus.","w":["Fleming discovered penicillin.","Peter Piot is linked to Ebola in another lecture.","Koch is not named in this lecture.","Pasteur is not named in this lecture."],"k":"Ehrlich coined \"chemotherapy\".","rn":[["","Chemotherapy","Chemotherapy","Use of synthetic chemicals capable of destroying infectious agents (Ehrlich Definition)"],["","Who coined the term \"chemotherapy\"?","Who coined the term \"chemotherapy\"?","Ehrlich"]]},{"id":41101,"t":"Antibiotics","s":"Which infections was penicillin described as effective against when Fleming discovered it?","o":["Gonorrhoea, meningitis, diphtheria and pneumonia","Influenza, the common cold and other viral respiratory infections","HIV and hepatitis","Malaria and sleeping sickness","Thrush and athlete's foot"],"e":"Fleming discovered penicillin, which was effective against gonorrhoea, meningitis, diphtheria and pneumonia and not toxic to humans at a therapeutic dose. Before antibiotics, infectious diseases were the leading cause of death.","w":["These are viral infections.","These are viral infections.","These are protozoal infections.","These are fungal infections."],"k":"Penicillin: gonorrhoea, meningitis, diphtheria, pneumonia.","rn":[["","Features of Penicillin","Features of Penicillin","Effective against gonorrhea, meningitis, diphteria, pneumonia; Not toxic to humans"]]},{"id":41102,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"A child with a severe Gram-negative infection is considered for a quinolone. Which caution applies?","o":["Quinolones need caution in children and have several interactions","Quinolones are completely contraindicated in all adults but are safe in every child","Quinolones stain teeth like tetracyclines","Quinolones turn saliva orange","Quinolones are bacteriostatic and fail in children"],"e":"Quinolones inhibit DNA gyrase, causing rapid bacterial death, with a mostly Gram-negative spectrum. The lecture lists several interactions, caution in children and possible development of resistant bacteria.","w":["They are used in adults.","Tooth staining is a tetracycline effect.","Orange saliva is rifampicin.","They cause rapid bacterial death."],"k":"Quinolones: caution in children, interactions.","rn":[["","What are the important cautions with quinolones?","What are the important cautions with quinolones?","Several interactions; Caution in children; Can lead to resistant bacteria"]]},{"id":41103,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"Which pairing of drug and characteristic adverse effect is correct?","o":["Rifampicin: orange saliva, tears and sweat","Amoxicillin: ototoxicity and hearing loss with long courses","Vancomycin: tooth staining","Tetracycline: arrhythmias","Clarithromycin: chelation of calcium"],"e":"Rifampicin: orange secretions and CYP450 induction. Amoxicillin: allergy and commensal damage. Vancomycin: nephro- and ototoxicity. Tetracyclines: phototoxicity and chelation (teeth, bone). Clarithromycin: nausea, diarrhoea, arrhythmias.","w":["Ototoxicity is vancomycin and aminoglycosides.","Tooth staining is tetracyclines.","Arrhythmias are clarithromycin.","Chelation is tetracyclines."],"k":"Match each antibiotic to its signature adverse effect.","rn":[["","Which body fluids does rifampicin turn orange?","Which body fluids does rifampicin turn orange?","Saliva; Tears; Sweat"]]},{"id":41104,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which pairing of drug and mechanism is NOT correct?","o":["Clarithromycin: inhibits DNA gyrase","Amoxicillin: inhibits cell wall synthesis","Vancomycin: binds D-Ala-D-Ala, blocking wall formation","Rifampicin: binds RNA polymerase","Trimethoprim: blocks dihydrofolate reductase"],"e":"Clarithromycin inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit; DNA gyrase is the quinolone target. The other pairings are correct: amoxicillin inhibits cell wall synthesis, vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala, rifampicin binds RNA polymerase and trimethoprim blocks dihydrofolate reductase.","w":["Amoxicillin does inhibit cell wall synthesis.","Vancomycin does bind D-Ala-D-Ala.","Rifampicin does bind RNA polymerase.","Trimethoprim does block dihydrofolate reductase."],"k":"Clarithromycin acts on 50S, not DNA gyrase.","rn":[["","Clarithromycin: mechanism of action?","Clarithromycin: mechanism of action?","Inhibits protein synthesis; Bacterial ribosome 50S subunit"],["","Which enzyme do quinolones block?","Which enzyme do quinolones block?","DNA gyrase"]]},{"id":41105,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"A patient with MRSA bacteraemia needs IV therapy. Which drug fits the lecture?","o":["Vancomycin","Amoxicillin","Penicillin V","Oral vancomycin","Metronidazole"],"e":"Vancomycin is used intravenously for serious Gram-positive organisms that produce β-lactamases or are not responding to other treatments, and is active against many resistant strains including MRSA. Oral vancomycin is not absorbed and is used only for C. difficile in the gut.","w":["MRSA is resistant to meticillin and β-lactams such as amoxicillin.","Penicillin V is a β-lactam.","Oral vancomycin is not absorbed into the blood.","Metronidazole targets anaerobes."],"k":"MRSA bacteraemia: IV vancomycin.","rn":[["","Vancomycin: activity?","Vancomycin: activity?","Only Gram positive; Many resistant strains incl. MRSA"],["","Why is oral vancomycin used for Clostridium difficile?","Why is oral vancomycin used for Clostridium difficile?","Not absorbed; Stays in the gut"]]},{"id":41106,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Which drug pair would be most worrying to prescribe together for a patient already on a drug that prolongs cardiac conduction?","o":["Adding clarithromycin","Adding amoxicillin","Adding clavulanic acid","Adding trimethoprim","Adding penicillin V"],"e":"Clarithromycin may alter cardiac conduction (arrhythmias), so it should not be combined with other drugs that also affect heart rhythm. Always check interactions (e.g. in the BNF) before adding an antibiotic.","w":["Amoxicillin is not listed for cardiac conduction effects.","Clavulanic acid is not listed for this.","Trimethoprim is not listed for this.","Penicillin V is not listed for this."],"k":"Clarithromycin + other QT/rhythm drugs: avoid.","rn":[["","Why avoid giving clarithromycin with other drugs that cause arrhythmia?","Why avoid giving clarithromycin with other drugs that cause arrhythmia?","Clarithromycin may alter cardiac conduction; Arrhythmia risk adds up"],["","What are the 2 adverse effects of clarithromycin?","What are the 2 adverse effects of clarithromycin?","Nausea and diarrhoea; Altered cardiac conduction (arrhythmias)"]]},{"id":41107,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which class and group are matched correctly with their spectrum?","o":["Glycopeptides: Gram positive only","Glycopeptides: Gram negative only","Quinolones: Gram positive only","Macrolides: Gram negative only","Aminopenicillins: narrow spectrum"],"e":"Glycopeptides (vancomycin, teicoplanin, telavancin) are only active against Gram positives. Quinolones are mostly Gram negative, also Gram positive and anaerobes. Macrolides act on Gram positives, Gram negatives and cell-wall-deficient bacteria. Aminopenicillins (amoxicillin, ampicillin) are broader spectrum.","w":["Glycopeptides are Gram positive only.","Quinolones are mostly Gram negative.","Macrolides cover both groups and cell-wall-deficient bacteria.","Aminopenicillins have a broader spectrum."],"k":"Glycopeptides: Gram positive only.","rn":[["","Glycopeptides are only active against what?","Glycopeptides are only active against what?","Gram positive bacteria"]]},{"id":41108,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"Why can β-lactams NOT treat Mycoplasma, whereas clarithromycin can?","o":["Mycoplasma is cell-wall deficient, and β-lactams act on the cell wall","Mycoplasma produces large amounts of β-lactamase that destroys the drug","Mycoplasma has 80S ribosomes","Mycoplasma is a virus","Mycoplasma lives only in the gut"],"e":"β-lactams act on cell wall synthesis. Mycoplasma is a cell-wall-deficient bacterium, so it has no target for β-lactams. Clarithromycin inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit and is active against cell-wall-deficient bacteria such as Mycoplasma.","w":["The issue is the missing wall, not just β-lactamase.","Bacteria have 70S ribosomes.","Mycoplasma is a bacterium.","Its site is not the reason."],"k":"No cell wall → β-lactams useless; macrolides work.","rn":[["","Which cell wall deficient bacterium does clarithromycin treat?","Which cell wall deficient bacterium does clarithromycin treat?","Mycoplasma"]]},{"id":41109,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"A patient's infection is caused by bacteria that pump the antibiotic out of the cell. Which resistance mechanism is this?","o":["Enhanced export (efflux pumps)","Reduced uptake of the antibiotic across the cell envelope","Inactivation by β-lactamase","Alteration of the target","Alternative pathways"],"e":"The six resistance mechanisms: inactivation or modification (e.g. β-lactamases); altered prodrug-activating enzymes; altered target; reduced uptake; enhanced export (efflux pumps); alternative pathways. Pumping the drug out is enhanced export.","w":["Reduced uptake keeps the drug out in the first place.","β-lactamase destroys β-lactams.","Target alteration stops binding.","Alternative pathways bypass a blocked step."],"k":"Pumping drug out = efflux.","rn":[["","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on entry or pathways?","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on entry or pathways?","Reduced uptake; Enhanced export (efflux pumps); Alternative pathways"]]},{"id":41110,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"A bacterium becomes resistant to trimethoprim by using a different route to make the folate it needs. Which mechanism is this?","o":["Development of alternative pathways","Enhanced export of trimethoprim by efflux pumps in the membrane","Inactivation of the antibiotic","Reduced uptake","Altered prodrug activation"],"e":"Development of alternative pathways is one of the six resistance mechanisms: the bacterium goes around the blocked step instead of through it.","w":["Efflux pumps export the drug.","Inactivation destroys the drug.","Reduced uptake keeps it out.","Altered activation applies to prodrugs."],"k":"Bypassing the blocked step = alternative pathway.","rn":[["","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on entry or pathways?","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on entry or pathways?","Reduced uptake; Enhanced export (efflux pumps); Alternative pathways"]]},{"id":41111,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","a":["selective toxicity"],"s":"What term describes an antibiotic harming the microbe but not the host?","e":"Selective toxicity: the drug attacks something bacteria have and we do not, giving a therapeutic effect with low toxicity.","k":"Selective toxicity = harm microbe, spare host.","rn":[["","What is meant by Selective Toxicity?","What is meant by Selective Toxicity?","Harms the microorganism; Little toxicity to the host"]]},{"id":41112,"t":"Mechanisms of Action","a":["bactericidal"],"s":"What term describes an antibiotic that kills bacteria?","e":"Bactericidal antibiotics kill bacteria, such as β-lactams, aminoglycosides, metronidazole and rifampicin. Bacteriostatic drugs stop growth.","k":"Bactericidal = kills bacteria.","rn":[["","What is meant by Bactericidal?","What is meant by Bactericidal?","Kills bacteria"],["","Aminoglycosides: bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Aminoglycosides: bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Bactericidal"],["","Metronidazole: bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Metronidazole: bacteriostatic or bactericidal?","Bactericidal"]]},{"id":41113,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","a":["prodrug","pro-drug"],"s":"What term describes a drug that is inactive until metabolised to its active form, like metronidazole?","e":"A prodrug is inactive until metabolised to its active form. Metronidazole is a prodrug that only anaerobes can activate.","k":"Prodrug = activated by metabolism.","rn":[["","Why does metronidazole only work on anaerobes?","Why does metronidazole only work on anaerobes?","Prodrug; Only anaerobes activate it"]]},{"id":41114,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","a":["beta-lactamase","β-lactamase","beta lactamase","penicillinase","beta-lactamases"],"s":"Name the bacterial enzyme that hydrolyses the β-lactam ring, making the antibiotic inactive.","e":"β-lactamase catalyses hydrolysis of the β-lactam ring, the main mechanism of resistance to β-lactams. Clavulanic acid inhibits many β-lactamases.","k":"β-lactamase hydrolyses the β-lactam ring.","rn":[["","Name a beta-lactamase inhibitor.","Name a beta-lactamase inhibitor.","Clavulanic acid"],["","In the penicillin and cephalosporin nuclei, what does beta-lactamase cleave?","In the penicillin and cephalosporin nuclei, what does beta-lactamase cleave?","The beta-lactam ring"],["","How is beta-lactamase resistance overcome?","How is beta-lactamase resistance overcome?","Beta-lactamase inhibitors"]]},{"id":41115,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"A doctor considers whether an antibiotic is ideal for a patient on many medicines. Which ideal characteristic is most relevant?","o":["No interference with other drugs","Slow emergence of resistance in the patient's bacteria over the course","Selective toxicity","Activity in several body sites","Use as prophylaxis"],"e":"One ideal characteristic is no interference with other drugs: always check interactions before adding an antibiotic. Others are selective toxicity, slow emergence of resistance and non-toxicity to the host.","w":["Resistance is not the key issue for interactions.","Selective toxicity concerns hitting bacteria not host.","Activity in several sites is a feature, not an ideal.","Prophylaxis is a use, not an ideal."],"k":"Check interactions: \"no interference with other drugs\".","rn":[["","What are the 4 ideal characteristics of an antibiotic?","What are the 4 ideal characteristics of an antibiotic?","Selective toxicity; Slow emergence of resistance; Non-toxic to host; No interference with other drugs"]]},{"id":41116,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"Which statement about why there are so many penicillins is correct?","o":["They differ in spectrum, resistance and pharmacokinetics; species have different transpeptidases","They are all chemically identical but sold under different brand names","Each penicillin targets a different human enzyme","They differ only in colour","They were all discovered in the same year by chance"],"e":"There are many penicillins because they have different spectrum, resistance and pharmacokinetic characteristics, and different bacterial species have different transpeptidases. For example, Pseudomonas needs special antipseudomonal penicillins such as piperacillin.","w":["They differ in important properties.","They target bacterial transpeptidases, not human enzymes.","Colour is irrelevant.","They were developed over time."],"k":"Penicillins differ in spectrum, resistance and pharmacokinetics.","rn":[["","Why are there so many penicillins?","Why are there so many penicillins?","Different spectrum, resistance, pharmacokinetics; Different transpeptidases per species"]]},{"id":41117,"t":"Mechanisms of Action","s":"Which combination is a correct summary of the four main modes of antibiotic action?","o":["Cell wall, protein synthesis, genetic material, bacterial metabolism","Nucleus, mitochondria, Golgi apparatus and lysosomes of the bacterium","Antibodies, complement, T cells, B cells","Spores, capsules, flagella, pili","Kidneys, liver, lungs, heart"],"e":"The main modes of action are: inhibiting the cell wall, inhibiting protein synthesis, altering genetic material (DNA/RNA) and altering bacterial metabolism (folate).","w":["These are eukaryotic organelles, not antibiotic targets.","These are immune components.","These are not the listed targets.","These are human organs."],"k":"Modes: wall, protein, DNA/RNA, metabolism.","rn":[["","What are the 4 main modes of antibiotic action?","What are the 4 main modes of antibiotic action?","Inhibit cell wall; Inhibit protein synthesis; Alter genetic material; Alter bacterial metabolism"]]},{"id":41118,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"A patient with an anaerobic gut infection is pregnant. Which reasoning is most consistent with the lecture?","o":["Avoid metronidazole because it is potentially teratogenic","Use metronidazole because it is safe in pregnancy","Use a tetracycline such as doxycycline, because it is safe in pregnancy","Use rifampicin because it is anaerobe-specific","Use vancomycin IV for all anaerobes"],"e":"Metronidazole is a prodrug activated by anaerobes, bactericidal, and considered potentially mutagenic, carcinogenic and teratogenic; the lecturer says it should not be prescribed in pregnancy. Tetracyclines should also be avoided in pregnancy.","w":["It is considered potentially teratogenic.","Tetracyclines should be avoided in pregnancy.","Rifampicin targets mycobacteria, not anaerobes specifically.","IV vancomycin is for serious Gram-positive infections."],"k":"Pregnancy: avoid metronidazole and tetracyclines.","rn":[["","Metronidazole is considered potentially what 3 things?","Metronidazole is considered potentially what 3 things?","Mutagenic; Carcinogenic; Teratogenic"]]},{"id":41119,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"A patient on long-term trimethoprim is given folinic acid. Why, according to the beyond-LO note?","o":["To prevent folate deficiency (megaloblastic anaemia)","To prevent nephrotoxicity from long-term trimethoprim use","To stop orange discolouration of urine","To reverse penicillin allergy","To increase β-lactamase activity"],"e":"Trimethoprim inhibits folate's role in nucleotide synthesis (an antimetabolite) and can cause folate deficiency. Beyond the learning outcomes, folate deficiency can cause megaloblastic anaemia, prevented in long-term treatment by giving folinic acid.","w":["Trimethoprim's issue is folate, not kidneys.","Orange urine relates to rifampicin.","Folinic acid does not treat allergy.","β-lactamase is unrelated."],"k":"Long-term trimethoprim: folinic acid prevents folate deficiency.","rn":[["","What can folate deficiency cause?","What can folate deficiency cause?","Megaloblastic anaemia"],["","How can folate deficiency be prevented in long-term trimethoprim treatment?","How can folate deficiency be prevented in long-term trimethoprim treatment?","Give folinic acid"]]},{"id":41120,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"A GP gives a patient a broad-spectrum antibiotic for a mild infection that a narrow-spectrum drug would treat. Which risk increases most?","o":["Resistance and side effects, including commensal damage","Selective toxicity","The patient's own immunity to the infection after treatment","Absorption of the drug","Speed of culture results"],"e":"Broader spectrum means an increased risk of resistance and side effects. Broad-spectrum drugs also hit good commensal bacteria, which can allow opportunistic infections such as Candida or C. difficile.","w":["Selective toxicity is not increased by broader spectrum.","Spectrum does not change immunity.","Absorption is a separate property.","Culture speed is unaffected."],"k":"Broad spectrum → more resistance and side effects.","rn":[["","What is the downside of broad spectrum antibiotics?","What is the downside of broad spectrum antibiotics?","Increased risk of resistance; Increased side effects"]]},{"id":41121,"t":"Clinical Application","s":"An 80-year-old woman with chronic leg oedema from heart failure develops red, hot, swollen skin over her lower leg after a visit to the chiropodist. Her notes record a penicillin allergy. She is started on an alternative antibiotic. Three days later her leg has improved and culture confirms the organism is sensitive, but she now has a sore mouth with white plaques on her palate.<br><br>Which antibiotic was most likely chosen, and what explains her mouth?","o":["Clarithromycin; it killed commensals, letting Candida overgrow","Amoxicillin; an allergic rash has developed inside her mouth from the penicillin","Vancomycin by mouth; ototoxicity has spread to the mouth and throat","Gentamicin; nephrotoxicity has caused white plaques in the mouth","Clarithromycin; it has caused an arrhythmia that presents with mouth pain"],"e":"Step 1: the most common pathogen in cellulitis is Streptococcus pyogenes, always susceptible to penicillins, but she is allergic, so a macrolide (clarithromycin) with a similar spectrum is used. Step 2: antibiotics select out commensals, so Candida, probably a commensal, overgrows and becomes a pathogen: oral thrush.","w":["She is allergic to penicillin, so amoxicillin would not be chosen.","Oral vancomycin is used for Clostridium difficile; ototoxicity affects hearing.","Gentamicin is not described for cellulitis, and nephrotoxicity affects the kidneys.","Arrhythmia is a clarithromycin risk but does not explain white oral plaques."],"k":"Penicillin allergy → macrolide; antibiotics → Candida overgrowth.","rn":[["","Image card: What is this? Why can it follow antibiotics?","What is this? Why can it follow antibiotics?","Oral thrush (Candida albicans); Antibiotics select out commensals"],["","In the oral thrush case, what did Candida change from and to?","In the oral thrush case, what did Candida change from and to?","Commensal to pathogen"]]},{"id":41122,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"A patient with a chest infection is given amoxicillin but does not improve after several days. The organism is found to produce an enzyme that hydrolyses the beta-lactam ring. The doctor switches to a combination product.<br><br>Which combination is most appropriate, and how does it work?","o":["Co-amoxiclav; clavulanic acid inhibits beta-lactamase, protecting amoxicillin","Co-trimoxazole; it blocks two steps of the folate pathway","Amoxicillin with vancomycin; vancomycin destroys the bacterial beta-lactamase","Amoxicillin with gentamicin; gentamicin repairs the damaged beta-lactam ring","Co-amoxiclav; clavulanic acid kills the bacteria directly"],"e":"Step 1: the main beta-lactam resistance is bacterial beta-lactamases, which hydrolyse the beta-lactam ring and inactivate the antibiotic; this is one reason a patient may not improve on amoxicillin. Step 2: co-amoxiclav is amoxicillin + clavulanic acid, a beta-lactamase inhibitor that does not kill bacteria itself but blocks the enzyme so amoxicillin can work.","w":["Co-trimoxazole targets folate synthesis, not beta-lactamase.","Vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala; it does not destroy beta-lactamase.","Gentamicin does not repair beta-lactams.","Clavulanic acid does not kill bacteria itself."],"k":"Beta-lactamase → add clavulanic acid (co-amoxiclav).","rn":[["","Co-amoxiclav = ?","Co-amoxiclav = ?","Amoxicillin + clavulanic acid"],["","How does clavulanic acid help amoxicillin?","How does clavulanic acid help amoxicillin?","Blocks beta-lactamase; Amoxicillin can then work"],["","Name a beta-lactamase inhibitor.","Name a beta-lactamase inhibitor.","Clavulanic acid"]]},{"id":41123,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"A 68-year-old in hospital develops diarrhoea due to Clostridium difficile after a course of broad-spectrum antibiotics. A junior doctor plans intravenous vancomycin. The consultant instead prescribes oral vancomycin.<br><br>Why is the oral route preferred here?","o":["It is not absorbed from the gut, so it stays where the infection is","Oral vancomycin is absorbed better than IV and reaches the blood faster","IV vancomycin cannot act on Gram positive bacteria","Oral vancomycin is not nephrotoxic because it is excreted in bile","The gut converts vancomycin into a beta-lactam"],"e":"Step 1: vancomycin is a very large molecule with very low GI absorption; it is usually given IV for serious Gram positive infections (e.g. MRSA). Step 2: because it is not absorbed, oral vancomycin stays in the gut, where Clostridium difficile causes diarrhoea: an example of selective toxicity by site.","w":["It has very low GI absorption.","Vancomycin is only active against Gram positives, by either route.","Vancomycin is excreted in urine.","Vancomycin is a glycopeptide, not a beta-lactam."],"k":"Oral vancomycin for C. difficile: not absorbed, acts in the gut.","rn":[["","Why is oral vancomycin used for Clostridium difficile?","Why is oral vancomycin used for Clostridium difficile?","Not absorbed; Stays in the gut"]]},{"id":41124,"t":"Cell Wall Inhibitors","s":"A 55-year-old with a serious MRSA infection is on intravenous vancomycin. A colleague suggests adding gentamicin for broader cover. The patient's kidney function is already reduced, and he reports new ringing in his ears.<br><br>What is the main concern with adding gentamicin?","o":["Both are nephrotoxic and ototoxic, so the damage adds up","Gentamicin will block vancomycin binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Gentamicin induces CYP450, lowering vancomycin levels","Both act on the 50S subunit, so they compete","Gentamicin turns saliva orange, hiding signs of bleeding"],"e":"Step 1: vancomycin is nephrotoxic and ototoxic with a narrow therapeutic window (dosed by blood levels), and should not be given with other ototoxic or nephrotoxic drugs. Step 2: gentamicin (an aminoglycoside) is also nephrotoxic and ototoxic, so giving them together adds the damage, even though they work by different mechanisms; check the BNF for interactions.","w":["They act by different mechanisms and do not block each other.","CYP450 induction is a rifampicin property.","Vancomycin acts on the cell wall; aminoglycosides act on 30S.","Orange saliva is a rifampicin effect."],"k":"Vancomycin + gentamicin: additive nephro- and ototoxicity.","rn":[["","Why not give vancomycin with gentamicin?","Why not give vancomycin with gentamicin?","Both nephrotoxic and ototoxic"],["","Vancomycin: interactions?","Vancomycin: interactions?","Other ototoxic or nephrotoxic drugs"],["","Why must gentamicin levels be monitored?","Why must gentamicin levels be monitored?","Nephrotoxic and ototoxic; Plasma levels are checked"]]},{"id":41125,"t":"Protein Synthesis Inhibitors","s":"A 6-year-old has an infection that would respond to doxycycline. The parent asks why the doctor refuses to prescribe it, and mentions the child's older relative has yellow-brown stained teeth after a similar drug as a child.<br><br>What is the best explanation for the doctor's decision?","o":["Tetracyclines chelate calcium and deposit in growing teeth and bone","Tetracyclines are bactericidal and kill the child's commensal teeth bacteria","Tetracyclines bind 50S ribosomes in human tooth cells","Tetracyclines are nephrotoxic in children under 9","Tetracyclines are inactive when given orally"],"e":"Step 1: tetracyclines (e.g. doxycycline) chelate (bind) metal ions such as calcium, so they are deposited where calcium goes. Step 2: in children under 9 years (growing age) this causes permanent yellow-brown tooth discolouration and inhibits bone growth, so they are avoided in young children and in pregnancy.","w":["Tetracyclines are bacteriostatic, and staining is from chelation.","Tetracyclines act on bacterial 30S subunits.","Nephrotoxicity is a feature of aminoglycosides and vancomycin.","Tetracyclines are not described as inactive orally."],"k":"Tetracyclines: chelate calcium → teeth/bone; avoid under 9 and in pregnancy.","rn":[["","Why is tetracycline avoided under 9 years old?","Why is tetracycline avoided under 9 years old?","Binds calcium (chelation); Growing teeth and bones need calcium"]]},{"id":41126,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"A 35-year-old starts treatment for tuberculosis. A week later she rushes to her GP, worried that her tears and saliva are orange and her urine looks red. She also takes another medicine whose level has unexpectedly fallen.<br><br>Which drug and properties explain both findings?","o":["Rifampicin: excreted in body fluids, and a strong CYP450 inducer","Metronidazole: a prodrug activated only by anaerobes","Vancomycin: nephrotoxic, causing blood in the urine","Clarithromycin: alters cardiac conduction","Ciprofloxacin: inhibits DNA gyrase in human cells"],"e":"Step 1: rifampicin binds RNA polymerase and is bactericidal against mycobacteria (TB). It is excreted in saliva, urine, sweat and tears, colouring them orange; it is not blood. Step 2: it is a strong inducer of CYP450, speeding up the breakdown of other drugs and lowering their levels.","w":["Metronidazole does not cause orange fluids or CYP450 induction here.","The red urine is drug colouring, not blood.","Arrhythmia does not explain these findings.","Quinolones target bacterial DNA gyrase."],"k":"Rifampicin: orange secretions + CYP450 induction.","rn":[["","Which body fluids does rifampicin turn orange?","Which body fluids does rifampicin turn orange?","Saliva; Tears; Sweat"],["","Rifampicin: metabolic interaction?","Rifampicin: metabolic interaction?","Strong induction of CYP450"]]},{"id":41127,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"A lab compares sulfonamide alone, trimethoprim alone, and the two together against the same bacterium. Each drug alone only stops growth. Together, the effect is much greater than the two added, and the bacteria may be killed.<br><br>What explains this?","o":["They block two different steps of tetrahydrofolate synthesis: synergy","They both bind the 30S ribosomal subunit at the same site","Trimethoprim inactivates beta-lactamase for the sulfonamide","Sulfonamide makes the cell wall permeable to trimethoprim","They both inhibit DNA gyrase, doubling the effect"],"e":"Step 1: co-trimoxazole is a sulfonamide + trimethoprim. Both act in the same pathway (synthesis of tetrahydrofolate, needed for thymidylate and DNA) at different stages: sulfonamides block dihydropteroate synthetase and trimethoprim blocks dihydrofolate reductase. Step 2: alone each is bacteriostatic; together they are synergistic (greater than the sum), may be bactericidal, and resistance is not common.","w":["They are antimetabolites, not ribosome inhibitors.","Neither acts on beta-lactamase.","They act on folate synthesis, not wall permeability.","DNA gyrase is the quinolone target."],"k":"Co-trimoxazole: two blocks in one pathway = synergy.","rn":[["","What pathway do sulfonamide and trimethoprim block?","What pathway do sulfonamide and trimethoprim block?","Tetrahydrofolate synthesis; At different stages"]]},{"id":41128,"t":"Principles of Antibiotic Treatment","s":"A 50-year-old arrives in A&amp;E with a serious infection. The organism is unknown, and culture results will take days. The doctor starts an antibiotic covering the most likely bacteria, sends a sample, and later changes the drug.<br><br>Which sequence describes this approach correctly?","o":["Empirical cover now; switch to a targeted drug when sensitivities return","Wait for culture before giving any antibiotic, then choose","Give the broadest antibiotic for the full course regardless of results","Give prophylaxis now, then therapeutic treatment once the patient recovers","Choose any narrow drug, as all antibiotics cover all bacteria"],"e":"Step 1: empirical treatment is antibiotic therapy started before the causative organism is identified, covering the likely bacteria; a culture sample is sent. Step 2: when the culture and sensitivity result returns, switch to the antibiotic that suits that bacterium. Broader spectrum increases resistance and side effects, so targeting matters.","w":["In a serious infection treatment starts before results.","Broader spectrum means more resistance and side effects.","Prophylaxis prevents infection; she already has one.","Antibiotic activity varies and is rarely single-organism specific."],"k":"Empirical first, then targeted on sensitivities.","rn":[["","After empirical treatment, what changes the antibiotic?","After empirical treatment, what changes the antibiotic?","Culture and sensitivity results"]]},{"id":41129,"t":"Genetic Material and Metabolism","s":"A pregnant woman develops an infection caused by anaerobic bacteria. A student suggests metronidazole because it is bactericidal against anaerobes.<br><br>Why is this choice a concern, according to the lecture?","o":["It is considered potentially teratogenic, so it should not be given in pregnancy","It cannot be activated by anaerobes and is therefore useless","It strongly induces CYP450 and turns body fluids orange","It is ototoxic and damages the fetus's hearing","It chelates calcium and stains the fetus's teeth"],"e":"Step 1: metronidazole is a prodrug: only anaerobic organisms metabolise it to its active form, whose metabolites are toxic to DNA (bactericidal). Step 2: it is considered potentially mutagenic, carcinogenic and teratogenic (may cause defects in the embryo or fetus), so the lecturer says it should not be prescribed in pregnancy.","w":["Anaerobes do activate it.","CYP450 induction and orange fluids are rifampicin effects.","Ototoxicity is a vancomycin/aminoglycoside effect.","Chelation is a tetracycline effect."],"k":"Metronidazole: anaerobe-activated prodrug; avoid in pregnancy.","rn":[["","Metronidazole is considered potentially what 3 things?","Metronidazole is considered potentially what 3 things?","Mutagenic; Carcinogenic; Teratogenic"]]},{"id":41130,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","s":"Three resistant bacterial isolates are studied. Isolate 1 has mutations in its ribosomes so a protein synthesis inhibitor no longer binds. Isolate 2 pumps the antibiotic out of the cell as soon as it enters. Isolate 3 makes an enzyme that hydrolyses the beta-lactam ring.<br><br>Which mechanisms are shown, in order?","o":["Altered target; enhanced efflux; inactivation of the antibiotic","Reduced uptake; alternative pathway; altered target","Inactivation; altered target; enhanced efflux","Alternative pathway; reduced uptake; altered prodrug activation","Enhanced efflux; inactivation; reduced uptake"],"e":"Step 1: ribosome mutations reducing binding of protein synthesis inhibitors are alteration of the target. Step 2: pumping the drug out is enhanced export (efflux pumps), and beta-lactamases hydrolysing the beta-lactam ring are inactivation or modification of the antibiotic. Other mechanisms are altered prodrug activation, reduced uptake and alternative pathways.","w":["Isolate 1 changes the target, not uptake.","Isolate 1 is target alteration, not inactivation.","These do not match the three isolates.","The order is wrong."],"k":"Resistance: destroy it, change target, keep out, pump out, bypass.","rn":[["","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on entry or pathways?","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on entry or pathways?","Reduced uptake; Enhanced export (efflux pumps); Alternative pathways"],["","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on the drug or target?","Which 3 resistance mechanisms act on the drug or target?","Inactivate or modify antibiotic; Alter pro-drug activating enzymes; Alter the target"],["","What is meant by Efflux Pump?","What is meant by Efflux Pump?","Pumps the antibiotic out of the cell"]]}]);
